====================================================================
DOMAIN: Philosophical Debates — Relativism, Power/Knowledge, Discontinuity, and the Subject
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-14
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 7, Medium: 5, Low: 2); 4 entries marked INCOMPLETE (no abstract resolvable)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, OpenAlex, CrossRef
SOURCE_ISSUES: Semantic Scholar was persistently rate-limited (HTTP 429) throughout this session; all searches relied on PhilPapers (via Brave), OpenAlex, and CrossRef instead. CORE was not queried directly given the same environment constraint noted by the orchestrator; OpenAlex and CrossRef fully substituted for abstract/metadata needs.
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain gathers the sharpest philosophical objections to, and defenses of, Foucault's
history-writing as a general epistemological and political project. Four intertwined
controversies structure the literature. First, the relativism/anti-realism charge: critics
from Charles Taylor to Robert Nola argue that Foucault's genealogical unmasking of truth as an
effect of power collapses into a self-undermining relativism that cannot account for its own
normative or evidential standing (the "performative self-contradiction" or self-refutation
objection). Second, the power/knowledge thesis reconfigures truth and objectivity themselves:
Foucault's claim that power and knowledge "directly imply one another" (Discipline and Punish)
is read by Habermasian critics as eliding the distinction between validity and power, producing
what Nancy Fraser called "normative confusion" — Foucault's genealogies presuppose the very
evaluative standards (freedom, domination, resistance) that his own theory of power seems to
rule out. The Foucault/Habermas debate — never directly joined before Foucault's death in 1984
but reconstructed extensively by Ingram, Freundlieb, King, Biebricher, and Amy Allen — is the
central arena for this dispute, pitting Habermas's communicative-rationalist normative
foundations against Foucault's refusal of transcendental grounding. Third, the discontinuity
thesis: Foucault's account of epistemes as historically bounded, internally coherent, and
separated by ruptures (The Order of Things) has been challenged both by historians of ideas
(Maclean, on the Renaissance episteme) and defended/refined by philosophers analyzing its formal
completeness (Cutrofello) and its relation to Husserlian and Kantian a priori structures
(Vaccarino Bremner). Fourth, the decentering of the subject: Foucault's structuralist-inflected
"death of man" thesis, most famously glossed by his teacher Georges Canguilhem, raises the
question of how historical explanation is possible once the constituting subject is replaced by
anonymous discursive and power formations — a question taken up by later Foucauldians (Han-Pile)
defending an ethics of self-practice against the charge that Foucault leaves no standpoint from
which a subject could resist or judge. Tremain's application of Foucauldian historicist
relativism to disability studies shows how these abstract debates translate into a live,
first-order methodological choice for genealogical inquiry outside intellectual history proper.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the conceptual vocabulary and the argumentative battle lines a
comprehensive review of Foucault's philosophy of history must adjudicate: whether his
historiography is self-undermining relativism, whether "power/knowledge" is compatible with
historical objectivity and critique, whether epistemic discontinuity is a coherent unit of
historical explanation, and whether a decentered subject can still be an agent of, or object
within, historical narrative. These are the meta-level questions that the primary texts
(Domain 1), archaeology/genealogy as method (Domain 2), and the empirical historians' critique
(Domain 4) all presuppose or provoke.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The literature search did not turn up a citable, DOI-verifiable stand-alone article by Habermas
himself directly engaging Foucault (his fullest response is the book chapter/lecture "Some
Questions Concerning the Theory of Power" in The Philosophical Discourse of Modernity and the
essay "Taking Aim at the Heart of the Present" in Hoy's Foucault: A Critical Reader); Habermas's
position is represented here through Ingram's comprehensive comparison and the secondary debate
literature (Freundlieb, King, Biebricher, Allen) rather than a primary Habermas journal article,
since no verifiable, non-fabricated bibliographic record could be located via the available
search tools. Coverage of the ontological status of "epistemes" beyond Maclean's historical
counter-case and Cutrofello's formal analysis remains thin; a systematic realist/anti-realist
treatment of Foucauldian discontinuity (comparable to Kuhn-paradigm debates) was not located in
this pass.

Four entries (Fraser 1985, Han-Pile 2016, Allen 2009, Cutrofello 2003) are marked INCOMPLETE
because no abstract could be resolved via Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex despite confirmed,
DOI-verified bibliographic records; these are excluded from synthesis per workflow convention
but retained in this file for reference-manager completeness.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize by controversy rather than chronology: (1) relativism/self-refutation (Taylor, Nola,
Han-Pile, Tremain), (2) power/knowledge and the Habermas debate (Fraser, Freundlieb, Ingram,
King, Biebricher, Allen), (3) discontinuity/episteme (Maclean, Cutrofello, Vaccarino Bremner),
(4) the decentered subject (Canguilhem, with Han-Pile and Allen bridging back into the
normativity debate). Note that many papers span two categories (e.g., Han-Pile links subject
and normativity; Allen links subject and Habermas), so cross-reference rather than force strict
partition.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Relativism/self-refutation critics: Taylor, Nola — 2 papers — argue Foucault's account of
  truth as power-effect is either incoherent or covertly relies on realist/normative resources
  it disavows.
- Foucauldian defenses against self-refutation: Han-Pile, Tremain — 2 papers — argue the
  demand for normative/epistemic foundations is itself question-begging against a genealogical,
  historicist project.
- Habermasian normative-deficit critique: Fraser, Freundlieb, King — 3 papers — Foucault's
  genealogies of power presuppose evaluative distinctions (domination vs. freedom) his theory
  cannot ground.
- Reconstructive/synthesizing accounts of the Foucault-Habermas debate: Ingram, Biebricher,
  Allen — 3 papers — seek productive convergence or diagnose the debate as resting on mutual
  misreading.
- Discontinuity/episteme analysis: Maclean, Cutrofello, Vaccarino Bremner — 3 papers — probe the
  historical accuracy, formal coherence, and Kantian/Husserlian lineage of Foucault's rupture-based
  historiography.
- Decentered subject: Canguilhem — 1 paper — the foundational philosophical gloss on the "death
  of man" thesis and its implications for historical explanation without a constituting subject.
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Influence, Legacy, and Foucault in the Theory of Historiography
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-14
PAPERS_FOUND: 13 total (High: 8, Medium: 5, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef, WebSearch/WebFetch (Foucault News)
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers Foucault's afterlife within the philosophy of history proper: his placement
relative to narrativist and analytic historiographical theory, the ongoing theorization of his
"history of the present" as a mode of critical historiography, and the diffusion of genealogy as
a live method well beyond Foucault's own corpus. Three clusters emerge. First, a placement
literature that reads Foucault against the canonical philosophy-of-history tradition: Thomas
Flynn's two-volume Sartre, Foucault, and Historical Reason (1997, 2005) is the most sustained
attempt to locate Foucault's practice of history within, and against, an existentialist/
phenomenological theory of historical reason inherited from Sartre, treating archaeology and
genealogy as a "poststructuralist mapping of history" rather than a species of Hayden White-style
narrativism. The 2024 Journal of the Philosophy of History special issue on Arendt and Foucault
(Neill, Owen) performs a parallel placement exercise from the analytic/political-theory side of
the field, using Arendt as a comparison case to ask what kind of "history" Foucauldian genealogy
actually is. Second, a "history of the present" / presentism cluster: the Fuggle, Lanci, and
Tazzioli edited volume Foucault and the History of Our Present (2015) reactivates Foucault's own
methodological self-description as an ongoing research program, while Hartog's Regimes of
Historicity (2015), although not a Foucault book, supplies the wider historiographical-theoretical
vocabulary ("presentism," "regime of historicity") against which Foucault's history-of-the-present
method is now routinely read. Irrera's 2024 Foucault Studies article extends this by tracing the
concept of actualité back through Foucault's newly published Le Discours philosophique, showing
the "philosophy of the present" reading is still generating new archival work forty years after
Foucault's death. Third, a genealogy-as-critique cluster documenting the method's translation into
critical theory, political philosophy, and decolonial theory: Geuss's foundational 2002 article
and Koopman and Matza's "Putting Foucault to Work" (2013) set the terms of debate about whether
genealogy is properly critical or merely descriptive; Amy Allen's 2014 commentary on Koopman's book
(covered as a monograph in Domain 2) presses the question of genealogy's normative/transcendental
commitments; Harcourt's 2024 "On critical genealogy" and Nigh and Erlenbusch-Anderson's 2020 study
of genealogy's travel into Castro-Gómez's decolonial theory show the method still actively
mutating and being contested. Lorenzini's The Force of Truth (2023) is the most recent major
monograph, reconstructing genealogy, critique, and parrhesia as a unified late-Foucauldian project
and thereby consolidating much of this literature's terms for future debate.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain closes the review by showing that Foucault's theory of history did not remain a
self-contained French intellectual episode but became a durable reference point and working method
across historiographical theory, critical theory, and political philosophy - while its exact
disciplinary status (Is it history? Historiographical theory? A species of narrativism? A method
transferable across contexts?) remains structurally contested, mirroring the ambiguity already
identified in Domain 4 (Megill) at the level of practice.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Direct, sustained comparison of Foucault with Ankersmit's narrativist theory of history is thin in
the discoverable literature (most comparative work pairs Foucault with White, already covered in
Domain 4, or with Arendt); this remains a comparatively underexplored pairing. Coverage of
Foucault's reception within Anglophone analytic philosophy of history proper (as opposed to
political theory and critical theory) is correspondingly limited to the 2024 JPH special issue.
Four entries (Hartog 2015, Neill 2024, Geuss 2002, Lorenzini 2023) could not be resolved to a
verifiable abstract through the available APIs and are marked INCOMPLETE; notes for these are
grounded in verified CrossRef/Semantic Scholar/OpenAlex bibliographic metadata and, for Hartog and
Neill, independently verified secondary descriptions (an American Historical Review book review for
Hartog; the special issue's published table of contents and editorial framing for Neill) rather than
publisher abstracts. One automatically retrieved NDPR "abstract" for Lorenzini's The Force of Truth
was found to be a mismatched review of an unrelated book (Grayling on truth and realism) and was
therefore discarded rather than retained.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Use Flynn's two volumes and the Neill/Owen JPH pieces together to construct the "where does
Foucault sit relative to narrativist/analytic philosophy of history" strand; use the Geuss-
Koopman/Matza-Allen-Harcourt-Nigh/Erlenbusch-Anderson-Lorenzini cluster to trace the genealogy-as-
critique debate as a continuous, still-unresolved argument about legitimacy and normativity rather
than a settled method; use Fuggle et al. and Hartog together to frame the historiographical
theorization of "history of the present" as presentism's philosophical respectable cousin.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Foucault as existentialist-cum-poststructuralist historical reason (Flynn): 2 papers - situates
  archaeology/genealogy as successor-critique to Sartrean historical reason, not narrativism.
- Foucault vs. Arendt as rival genealogists of modernity (Owen, Neill): 2 papers - uses a
  comparative case internal to twentieth-century political philosophy to test what "doing
  genealogy" as history requires.
- History of the present as historiographical program (Fuggle et al., Irrera): 2 papers - treats
  the "ontology of the present" as an active, extendable research method, not just a slogan.
- Genealogy as critique - legitimacy debate (Geuss, Koopman and Matza, Allen, Harcourt, Nigh and
  Erlenbusch-Anderson, Lorenzini): 6 papers - disputes whether/how genealogical history secures
  normative or critical purchase, and whether the method travels intact beyond its European origin.
====================================================================
}

@misc{foucault2013historymadness,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = "History of Madness",
    year = "2013",
    doi = "10.4324/9780203642603",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Foucault's 1961 doctoral thesis (per IEP, orig. Folie et déraison: Histoire de la folie à l'âge classique) traces the systematic exclusion, and later pathologization, of "madness" in Western Europe from the Renaissance through the birth of psychiatry, arguing that the exclusion of unreason is constitutive of, rather than incidental to, Enlightenment reason. Its method already deploys what Foucault later calls "archaeology": attention to discontinuities and epistemic breaks (drawing on Bachelard's "epistemological rupture" and Canguilhem's normal/pathological distinction) rather than a continuous narrative of progress. RELEVANCE: This is the founding text of Foucault's historiography and the first work in which he explicitly uses the word "archaeology" (in the original preface) to describe his method, per IEP. It establishes the pattern — discrete historical ruptures in the treatment of a given object — that structures his subsequent studies of the clinic and the human sciences. POSITION: Inaugural work of Foucauldian "archaeology"; explicitly modeled in style on Nietzsche's Birth of Tragedy (IEP) and on the Bachelard-Canguilhem tradition of historical epistemology.},
    keywords = "archaeology, madness, epistemic-rupture, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "When it was first published in France in 1961 as Folie et Déraison: Histoire de la Folie à l'âge Classique, few had heard of a thirty-four year old philosopher by the name of Michel Foucault. By the time an abridged English edition was published in 1967 as Madness and Civilization, Michel Foucault had shaken the intellectual world. This translation is the first English edition of the complete French texts of the first and second edition, including all prefaces and appendices, some of them unavailable in the existing French edition. History of Madness begins in the Middle Ages with vivid descriptions of the exclusion and confinement of lepers. Why, Foucault asks, when the leper houses were emptied at the end of the Middle Ages, were they turned into places of confinement for the mad? Why, within the space of several months in 1656, was one out of every hundred people in Paris confined? Shifting brilliantly from Descartes and early Enlightenment thought to the founding of the Hôpital Général in Paris and the work of early psychiatrists Philippe Pinel and Samuel Tuke, Foucault focuses throughout, not only on scientific and medical analyses of madness, but also on the philosophical and cultural values attached to the mad. He also urges us to recognize the creative and liberating forces that madness represents, brilliantly drawing on examples from Goya, Nietzsche, Van Gogh and Artaud. The History of Madness is an inspiring and classic work that challenges us to understand madness, reason and power and the forces that shape them.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{foucault1973birthclinic,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = "The Birth of the Clinic: An Archaeology of Medical Perception",
    year = "1973",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Foucault's 1963 study of the emergence of modern clinical medicine around the French Revolution proposes, per its preface, to analyze medical discourse "on its own terms," treating signifiers without reference to a fundamental reality or signified they supposedly represent — an explicit methodological tightening relative to History of Madness. The book centers on the emergence of the medical "gaze" as a historically specific mode of attentive, non-prejudicial clinical observation tied to new social and political demands. RELEVANCE: This is the first work in which "archaeology" appears in Foucault's subtitle, marking its transition from an incidental term to a named method (IEP), though the book's practice does not yet fully match its stated program — a tension IEP notes is only resolved in The Order of Things. POSITION: Transitional archaeological text; its declared discourse-only method is not yet fully realized in its actual historical analysis.},
    keywords = "archaeology, medical-gaze, discourse, Medium, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{foucault2005orderthings,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = "The Order of Things",
    year = "2005",
    doi = "10.4324/9780203996645",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Subtitled "An Archaeology of the Human Sciences," this 1966 work argues that Western knowledge has passed through discrete "epistemes" — stable, unspoken ensembles of rules governing what can be known in a given period — separated by two major ruptures, one at the start of the Classical age (17th century) and one at the start of modernity (turn of the 19th century). Foucault explicitly declines to explain why these epistemic breaks occur, restricting archaeology to describing what changed (IEP); the book's famous conclusion anticipates the "end of man" as an object of knowledge. RELEVANCE: This is the text in which archaeology first becomes a fully deliberate, named methodology (IEP) rather than an incidental term, making it the central primary source for Foucault's account of historical discontinuity and the historical a priori — concepts central to this domain's key questions about how Foucault characterizes his own historiographical enterprise. POSITION: Canonical statement of archaeology as description of epistemic rupture, independent of causal or institutional explanation.},
    keywords = "archaeology, episteme, discontinuity, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = {When one defines "order" as a sorting of priorities, it becomes beautifully clear as to what Foucault is doing here. With virtuoso showmanship, he weaves an intensely complex history of thought. He dips into literature, art, economics and even biology in The Order of Things, possibly one of the most significant, yet most overlooked, works of the twentieth century. Eclipsed by his later work on power and discourse, nonetheless it was The Order of Things that established Foucault's reputation as an intellectual giant. Pirouetting around the outer edge of language, Foucault unsettles the surface of literary writing. In describing the limitations of our usual taxonomies, he opens the door onto a whole new system of thought, one ripe with what he calls "exotic charm". Intellectual pyrotechnics from the master of critical thinking, this book is crucial reading for those who wish to gain insight into that odd beast called Postmodernism, and a must for any fan of Foucault.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{foucault1972archaeology,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = "The Archaeology of Knowledge",
    year = "1972",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Foucault's 1969 theoretical consolidation of the archaeological method is, per IEP, the only major work that is not itself a historical study — his most purely methodological text. It proposes to analyze discourse in terms of discrete "statements" and their relations, independent of any subject who transcends or produces them, and introduces the notion of the "archive" as the system governing which statements can appear; archaeology is redefined as the excavation of this archive. RELEVANCE: This is Foucault's most explicit self-description of the archaeological method and thus the primary text against which claims about archaeology's philosophical coherence (Tiisala, Krarup, Peters) must be checked; it retroactively reframes the method used in his three prior monographs. POSITION: Theoretical self-clarification of archaeology; asserts the "relative autonomy" of discourse against both transcendental subjectivity and reductive social/economic determination.},
    keywords = "archaeology, discourse, statement, archive, High, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{foucault2019nietzsche,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = "Nietzsche, Genealogy, History",
    year = "2019",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: First published in the 1971 volume Hommage à Jean Hyppolite (per PhilPapers bibliographic record), this essay adapts Nietzsche's genealogical vocabulary — distinguishing Ursprung (origin), Herkunft (descent), and Entstehung (emergence) — to argue against the search for pure origins in history. Genealogy instead traces the contingent, often violent "play of dominations" through which practices and values emerge, and calls for a "wirkliche Historie" (effective history) that abandons suprahistorical, continuous narratives in favor of attention to disparity, chance, and the body as the site on which history is inscribed. RELEVANCE: This essay is the primary methodological manifesto for Foucault's genealogical period and is the key text for this domain's question of how the archaeology/genealogy shift should be understood; secondary sources on this domain's list (Koopman, Manley, Bevir) take it as their central object. POSITION: Foundational statement of Foucauldian genealogy as a Nietzschean-derived, anti-teleological, anti-originary mode of history-writing.},
    keywords = "genealogy, Nietzsche, effective-history, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, publisher, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "1 . Genealogy i s gray, meticulous, and patiently documentary . It operates on a field of entangled and confused parchments, on d ocuments that have been scratched over and recopied many times . O n this basis, it i s obvious that Paul Reel was wrong to follow the English tendency in describing the history of morality in terms of a linear development-in reducing its entire history and genesis to an exclusive concern for utility . He assumed that words had kept their meaning, that desires still pointed in a single direction, and that ideas retained their logic; and he ig­ nored the fact that the world of speech and desires has known invasions, struggles, plundering, disguises, ploys. From these elements, however, genealogy retrieves an indispensable re­ straint: it must record the singularity of events outside of any monotonous finality; it must seek them in the most unpromising places, in what we tend to feel is without history-in sentiments, love, conscience, instincts; it must be sensitive to their recur­ rence, not in order to trace the gradual curve of their evolution, but to isolate the different scenes where they engaged in dif­ ferent roles . Finally, genealogy must define even those in­ stances when they are absent, the moment when they remained unrealized (Plato, at Syracuse, did not become Mohammed) . Genealogy, consequently, requires patience and a knowl­ edge of details, and it depends on a vast accumulation of source",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{foucault1977discipline,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = "Discipline and Punish",
    year = "1977",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: This 1975 study of the emergence of the modern prison argues that the prison functions, structurally, to produce criminality and recidivism, and situates the prison within a broader historical emergence of "discipline" — a form of power first developed in 17th-century professional soldiering and subsequently generalized across the factory, the school, and the army, aimed at producing controllable "docile bodies." Its historiographical innovation, per IEP, is the introduction of "power-knowledge" as a replacement for the Marxist category of ideology: knowledge is not distorted by power from outside but is constitutively bound up with it. RELEVANCE: This is Foucault's first genealogical monograph and the primary text through which the archaeology/genealogy relationship becomes concrete; Garland's exposition and critique in this domain's list is devoted specifically to assessing its historical method and evidentiary basis. POSITION: Genealogy applied to an institutional history; introduces power-knowledge as Foucault's central historiographical-cum-political category.},
    keywords = "genealogy, discipline, power-knowledge, docile-bodies, High, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{foucault1978sexuality,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = "The History of Sexuality, Volume 1: An Introduction",
    year = "1978",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Published in French in 1976 as La volonté de savoir, this volume argues against the "repressive hypothesis" that modernity has silenced discourse on sex, showing instead a proliferation of discourses compelling us to speak of sex. Its central historiographical move, per IEP, is to reject a "juridical," monarchical model of power as prohibition (the call "to cut off the head of the king" in political theory) in favor of a relational, productive, and strategic conception of power operating without a single locus or origin, introducing the concepts of biopower and biopolitics. RELEVANCE: This text extends the genealogical method of Discipline and Punish into a fully elaborated theory of power as historically dispersed and non-originary, directly bearing on this domain's question of how Foucault's substantive histories relate to his metahistorical claims about power, knowledge, and historical causation. POSITION: Mature statement of genealogy as history of power-relations; rejects sovereign/juridical models of power and historical causation in favor of a relational, strategic model.},
    keywords = "genealogy, biopower, power-relations, sexuality, High, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{foucault2003society,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = {"Society Must Be Defended": Lectures at the Collège de France, 1975--1976},
    year = "2003",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: These 1975-76 lectures, delivered in the same year as the writing of Discipline and Punish, offer a genealogy of the discourse of race war as an alternative model to the sovereign/juridical theory of politics, and contain, per IEP, "perhaps the clearest exposition of Foucault's thoughts on biopower." Foucault here explicitly historicizes his own genealogical method by tracing race-war discourse's own historical career, effectively offering (per Bernasconi's secondary analysis, referenced in this domain's PhilPapers search) an alternative genealogy of his own genealogical perspective to the one given in "Nietzsche, Genealogy, History." RELEVANCE: As a lecture course contemporaneous with Discipline and Punish and preceding The History of Sexuality Vol. 1, this text is a crucial bridge document for tracing continuity/discontinuity in Foucault's historiographical self-understanding across the mid-1970s, and for assessing whether his 1971 genealogical manifesto remains stable through his later practice. POSITION: Genealogy of biopower and race-war discourse; posthumously published lecture course central to reconstructing Foucault's mid-1970s historiographical self-understanding.},
    keywords = "genealogy, biopower, race, Collège-de-France, Medium, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{foucault1984enlightenment,
    author = "Foucault, Michel",
    title = "What is Enlightenment",
    year = "1984",
    howpublished = "\url{https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/709a2cb3447bc99eb6288500ced95e9eaca586f9}",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: In this late essay, written as a response to Kant's 1784 "Was ist Aufklärung?," Foucault characterizes his entire historiographical enterprise, retrospectively, as a "history of the present": an inquiry into "what we are" that takes the form not of a search for universal structures but of a critical, historical analysis of the limits imposed on us, undertaken as a possible "crossing-over" of those limits. He proposes a "critical ontology of ourselves" as an ethos and a philosophical attitude toward the present rather than a doctrine. RELEVANCE: This essay is the primary text in which Foucault most explicitly names and reflects on "history of the present" as a self-description of his method, making it central to this domain's question of how the substantive histories relate to metahistorical self-characterization; it directly grounds the secondary debate over the "history of the present" as a unifying thread across archaeology and genealogy (Roth, Baert). POSITION: Late methodological self-description; recasts the entire Foucauldian project as a Kantian-derived "history of the present" and critical ontology of the present.},
    keywords = "history-of-the-present, Kant, critique, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@article{roth1981history,
    author = "Roth, Michael S.",
    title = {Foucault's "History of the Present"},
    journal = "History and Theory",
    year = "1981",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Roth argues that in The Birth of the Clinic, The Order of Things, and Discipline and Punish, Foucault writes a "history of the present" in which archaeological description of past discursive formations is never separable from a critical, present-oriented political stance; the "history of the present" is, on this reading, a self-conscious field of power relations and political struggle rather than a neutral reconstruction of the past. RELEVANCE: One of the earliest and most influential attempts to name and characterize the unifying historiographical method running across Foucault's archaeological and early genealogical texts, directly addressing this domain's core question about how Foucault's substantive histories relate to his metahistorical self-description. POSITION: Continuity thesis — archaeology and genealogy share the same "history of the present" logic connecting historical description to present critique.},
    keywords = "history-of-the-present, archaeology, method, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi",
    abstract = "A pastime of growing popularity in recent years has been that of observing often furious activity of French intellectual scene. Fascinated by apparently revolutionary implications of approaching unconscious, text, primitives, superstructure/base, and in latest way, American scholars from Baltimore to Santa Cruz have been avidly following (often just a few steps behind) many twists and turns of their colleagues across sea. This following often takes form of a redefinition of what French are in terms that slide more easily through our own vocabularies than terms in which French themselves choose to define their projects. This paper will join growing tradition in attempting a redefinition of some of implications of Michel Foucault's historical works. It will attempt to answer question What is Foucault really doing? by examining relationship between the past and the in The Birth of Clinic, The Order of Things, and Discipline and Punish. I shall try to make sense of what Foucault means by writing a history of by showing connections between archaeology and criticism in these three works. Foucault himself tried to tell his readers, in part, what he had really been doing in his methodological work, The Archaeology of Knowledge (1969), and I shall make some use of his explanations in this paper. However, The Archaeology of Knowledge did not answer some major questions about Foucault's work; questions that have been raised generally in philosophy of history. The relationship between inquirer's present with which he is examining is placed in foreground by Foucault's archaeological project; I shall attempt to explicate this relationship as well as briefly to distinguish it from other approaches to writing of history.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{baert1998history,
    author = "Baert, Patrick",
    title = "Foucault's History of the Present as Self-Referential Knowledge Acquisition",
    year = "1998",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Baert argues that Foucault's "history of the present" should be understood as a form of self-referential knowledge acquisition: Foucault's histories are not neutral reconstructions of the past but interventions that are themselves constitutive of the present they analyze, a concern Baert traces as anticipated across Foucault's career and common to both his archaeological and genealogical periods. RELEVANCE: Directly answers this domain's question about continuity versus rupture across Foucault's corpus, arguing that archaeology and genealogy "have more in common than conventionally assumed" because both are directed toward this self-referential form of knowledge — a claim in productive tension with Koopman's "expansion" thesis. POSITION: Continuity thesis — locates a shared self-referential epistemology beneath the archaeology/genealogy distinction.},
    keywords = "history-of-the-present, self-reference, continuity, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Underlying this article is the conviction that social scientists typically take on board a too restrictive concept of knowledge acquisition. The paper propounds a new concept of knowledge acquisition, one which is self-referential (i.e. which affects one's presuppositions) and which draws upon the unfamiliar to reveal and undercut the familiar. The aim of this paper is twofold. First, it is to show that this concept of knowledge acqui sition is already anticipated by Foucault, that it is a major concern of his, and that it is a common thread throughout his work. Consequently, a new light can be thrown on both Foucault's archaeology and his genealogy: both are directed towards a self-referential form of knowledge, and as such the two periods are shown to have more in common than conventionally assumed. Second (and conversely), the aim of the paper is to elucidate this self-referential type of knowledge by showing how it is used by Foucault.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{koopman2008historiographical,
    author = "Koopman, Colin",
    title = "Foucault's Historiographical Expansion: Adding Genealogy to Archaeology",
    journal = "Journal of the Philosophy of History",
    year = "2008",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Koopman rereads Foucault's widely disputed mid-career shift from archaeology to genealogy as an "expansion" rather than an abandonment: genealogy adds two dimensions to Foucault's historiography — moving from analyzing single, isolable domains of practice (e.g., knowledge alone) to analyzing interactions among multiple domains (e.g., power/knowledge), and expanding archaeology's discursive focus to include nondiscursive practices. RELEVANCE: Offers the most precise and influential secondary account of exactly how the archaeology/genealogy transition should be characterized, directly engaging this domain's central question of whether the shift constitutes a rupture or continuity, and providing conceptual vocabulary ("expansion") that mediates between rupture and continuity readings. POSITION: Expansion thesis — genealogy retains and builds upon rather than replaces archaeology.},
    keywords = "genealogy, archaeology, expansion, continuity, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi",
    abstract = "Abstract This paper offers a rereading of Foucault's much-disputed mid-career historiographical shift to genealogy from his earlier archaeological analytic. Disputing the usual view that this shift involves an abandonment of an archaeological method that was then replaced by a genealogical method, I show that this shift is better conceived as a historiographical expansion. Foucault's work subsequent to this shift should be understood as invoking both genealogy and archaeology. The metaphor of expansion is helpful in clarifying what was involved in Foucault's historiographical shift. I describe two expansions at the heart of Foucault's move. First, Foucault went from analyzing singular isolable domains of practice (such as knowledge) to analyzing the interactions between multiple domains of practice (such as power/knowledge). Second, Foucault went from an analytic which relied on a single temporal category of rupture to an analytic which invoked the relations amongst multiple temporalities, including continuity alongside discontinuity in his subsequent analyses.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{tiisala2015keeping,
    author = "Tiisala, Tuomo",
    title = "Keeping It Implicit: A Defense of Foucault's Archaeology of Knowledge",
    year = "2015",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Tiisala defends Foucault's archaeological method against the influential charge, from Dreyfus and Rabinow, that it rests on an incoherent conception of the rules governing discursive practices. Tiisala argues that this criticism arises from misreading archaeology as a transcendental inquiry into the conditions of possibility of discourse, when it is in fact a "diagnostic" project that need not, and should not, render its governing rules explicit. RELEVANCE: Provides a sustained philosophical defense of the coherence of the archaeological method as Foucault describes it in The Archaeology of Knowledge, directly bearing on this domain's question of the relationship between Foucault's substantive archaeological histories and his metahistorical, methodological self-descriptions. POSITION: Defends archaeology's philosophical coherence against the Dreyfus/Rabinow transcendental-inquiry misreading.},
    keywords = "archaeology, method, Dreyfus-Rabinow-critique, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "ABSTRACT: This paper defends Michel Foucault's notion of archaeology of knowledge against the influential and putatively devastating criticism by Dreyfus and Rabinow that Foucault's archaeological project is based on an incoherent conception of the rules of the discursive practices it purports to study. I argue first that Foucault's considered view of these rules as simultaneously implicit and historically efficacious corresponds to a general requirement for the normative structure of a discursive practice. Then I argue that Foucault is entitled to that view despite the charges to the contrary by Dreyfus and Rabinow. I also explain in detail how the argument by Dreyfus and Rabinow arises from a misunderstanding of Foucault's archaeological project as transcendental inquiry, while archaeology of knowledge is, in fact, a diagnostic project. The result is a novel understanding of the notion of archaeology of knowledge that enables a reassessment of Foucault's philosophical work in connection with current debates regarding the relationship between reflection and practice in the structure of thought.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{krarup2021archaeological,
    author = "Krarup, Troels",
    title = "Archaeological Methodology: Foucault and the History of Systems of Thought",
    year = "2021",
    doi = "10.1177/0263276420984528",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Krarup contextualizes Foucault's archaeological methodology within the French intellectual field of "history of systems of thought" as it existed around 1960, arguing that existing accounts have failed to explain why Foucault called his method "archaeology" rather than simply "history," and situating his redevelopment of a pre-existing comparative-archaeological methodology (via Dumézil) within the post-Hegelian tradition of studying "problems" in intellectual history. RELEVANCE: Grounds Foucault's self-description of his Collège de France chair title ("History of Systems of Thought," per SEP) and his archaeological method in a specific, previously underexplored disciplinary lineage, directly relevant to this domain's question of how Foucault characterizes his own enterprise. POSITION: Situates archaeology within the French "history of systems of thought" tradition rather than treating it as sui generis.},
    keywords = "archaeology, history-of-systems-of-thought, disciplinary-context, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Existing accounts of Foucault’s archaeological methodology have not (a) contextualized the concept properly within the intellectual field of its emergence and (b) explained why it is called ‘archaeology’ and not simply ‘history’. Foucault contributed to the field of ‘history of systems of thought’ in France around 1960 by broadening its scope from the study of scientific and philosophical systems into systems of ‘knowledge’ in a wider sense. For Foucault, the term ‘archaeology’ provided a response to new methodological questions arising from this initiative. Archaeological methodology had already been developed into a distinct comparative approach for the study of linguistic and cultural systems, notably by Dumézil. Foucault redevised archaeological methodology for the post-Hegelian tradition of studying ‘problems’ prevalent in the history of systems of thought. The article thus furnishes the groundwork for a ‘sociological archaeology’ or ‘problem analysis’ that is not particularly dependent on Foucault as a social theorist of power.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{manley2025disunity,
    author = "Manley, Robin",
    title = "The Disunity in Genealogy: Foucault's Anti-Nietzschean Reading of History",
    year = "2025",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Through a close reading of "Nietzsche, Genealogy, History" alongside Nietzsche's second Untimely Meditation, Manley argues that Foucault's account of genealogy is in fact incompatible with, and not a faithful exposition of, Nietzsche's own more dialectical philosophy of history. Where Foucault presents an anti-metaphysical Nietzsche suspicious of teleological origins, Nietzsche's essay actually insists on the necessity of ahistorical, quasi-teleological figures as an antidote to corrosive historicism. RELEVANCE: Directly challenges the self-understanding Foucault offers in his own genealogical manifesto, showing a gap between Foucault's substantive historical practice (as governed by his stated Nietzschean genealogy) and the actual philosophy of history found in his avowed source — precisely the kind of tension this domain's key questions ask synthesis writers to track. POSITION: Critical reading — Foucault's genealogy is an alternative to, rather than an extension of, Nietzsche's philosophy of history.},
    keywords = "genealogy, Nietzsche, critique, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Contemporary critical scholarship takes Foucault’s genealogical work as a paradigm, and it is widely recognized that Foucault himself took Nietzsche as a methodological exemplar. Notably, Foucault’s landmark methodological text – ‘Nietzsche, Genealogy, History’ – is framed as a faithful exposition of Nietzsche. Contrary to Foucault’s framing, however, this article argues that his account of genealogy is incompatible with and anathema to Nietzsche’s philosophy of history. Through a close reading of Foucault’s essay alongside Nietzsche’s second Unfashionable Observation , it seeks to demonstrate that Foucault’s account of genealogy is premised on an inadequately one-sided reading of Nietzsche’s more dialectical account of history and the ahistorical. Whereas Foucault presents an anti-metaphysical Nietzsche suspicious of teleological origins and ahistorical forms, Nietzsche’s essay in fact insists on the necessity of such figures as an antidote to historicism. Reading Nietzsche against Foucault, this article investigates the conceptual foundations of the contemporary genealogical method, suggesting that Nietzsche may present an alternative – rather than precursor – to the Foucauldian paradigm.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{peters2021episteme,
    author = "Peters, Rik",
    title = "The Episteme and the Historical A Priori: On Foucault's Archaeological Method",
    year = "2021",
    abstract = "Interpreters of Michel Foucault's 1966 Les mots et les choses have often conflated the terms 'episteme' and 'historical a priori'. This article suggests that the two terms are entirely separate: while 'episteme' refers to the configuration of thought in a given historical period, 'historical a priori' refers to the conditions of unity for a certain field of science within a given period. In his use of the term 'historical a priori', Foucault is thus much closer to Husserl than has hitherto been appreciated. Keeping the two terms separated also sheds new light on the archaeological method that Foucault uses, showing that there is a procedure to get from an archive of texts to the reconstruction of an episteme.",
    abstract_source = "s2",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Peters argues that "episteme" and "historical a priori," frequently treated as synonyms by Foucault's interpreters, are in fact distinct concepts in The Order of Things: episteme names the overall configuration of thought in a period, while the historical a priori names the conditions of unity for a specific field of science, bringing Foucault's usage closer to Husserl's phenomenology than usually recognized. RELEVANCE: Offers a precise conceptual disambiguation of two central terms Foucault uses to characterize his own archaeological enterprise in The Order of Things, clarifying exactly what kind of "conditions of possibility" archaeology is meant to uncover — central to this domain's question of Foucault's self-characterization of archaeology. POSITION: Conceptual clarification — separates episteme from historical a priori and links the latter to Husserlian phenomenology.},
    keywords = "archaeology, episteme, historical-a-priori, Husserl, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{flynn2016experiences,
    author = "Flynn, Thomas R.",
    title = "Foucault on Experiences and the Historical A Priori: With Husserl in the Rearview Mirror of History",
    year = "2016",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Flynn examines Foucault's appropriation and transformation of the Husserlian "historical a priori" as a category for describing the changing conditions of possible experience across historical periods, arguing that Foucault retains a phenomenological debt to Husserl even while historicizing what Husserl treated as more nearly transcendental. RELEVANCE: Complements Peters' and Carr's work by tracing the phenomenological genealogy of one of Foucault's key metahistorical concepts, bearing directly on this domain's question of how Foucault's substantive histories (of the epistemes) relate to his metahistorical apparatus (the historical a priori). POSITION: Traces continuity between Husserlian phenomenology and Foucault's historicized "historical a priori."},
    keywords = "historical-a-priori, Husserl, phenomenology, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@article{bevir2008genealogy,
    author = "Bevir, Mark",
    title = "What is Genealogy?",
    journal = "Journal of the Philosophy of History",
    year = "2008",
    doi = "10.1163/187226308x335958",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Bevir offers a general metahistorical account of genealogy as a mode of historical explanation, addressing what kind of explanatory and critical claims genealogical histories (of the Nietzschean-Foucauldian type) can coherently make about the contingent emergence of practices, in contrast to both linear-causal history and teleological narrative. RELEVANCE: As part of a 2008 special issue of the Journal of the Philosophy of History devoted to genealogy (which also includes Koopman's contribution on Foucault's own historiographical expansion), this article supplies the general philosophy-of-history framework within which Foucault's specific genealogical practice can be assessed. POSITION: General metahistorical account of genealogy as an explanatory mode distinct from causal and teleological history.",
    keywords = "genealogy, philosophy-of-history, method, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages",
    abstract = "Abstract This paper offers a theory of genealogy, explaining its rise in the nineteenth century, its epistemic commitments, its nature as critique, and its place in the work of Nietzsche and Foucault. The crux of the theory is recognition of genealogy as an expression of a radical historicism, rejecting both appeals to transcendental truths and principles of unity or progress in history, and embracing nominalism, contingency, and contestability. In this view, genealogies are committed to the truth of radical historicism and, perhaps more provisionally, the truth of their own empirical content. Similarly, genealogies operate as denaturalizing critiques of ideas and practices that hide the contingency of human life behind formal ahistorical or developmental perspectives.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{garland1986discipline,
    author = "Garland, David",
    title = "Foucault's Discipline and Punish: An Exposition and Critique",
    year = "1986",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Garland provides a systematic exposition of the historical and analytical claims of Discipline and Punish — its account of the shift from sovereign, spectacular punishment to disciplinary, carceral power, and its introduction of "power-knowledge" — before subjecting the book's historical and explanatory adequacy to critique, questioning both its evidentiary basis and the coherence of its claims about the functions of the prison. RELEVANCE: Offers a close, critical reading of exactly what kind of history-writing Discipline and Punish practices and what explanatory commitments (functionalist, strategic, or otherwise) underlie its genealogical narrative, directly relevant to this domain's question about the relationship between Foucault's substantive histories and his metahistorical claims. POSITION: Sympathetic but critical exposition, questioning the historical and explanatory rigor of Foucault's genealogical method as practiced in Discipline and Punish.},
    keywords = "genealogy, discipline, method-critique, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "An abstract is not available for this content so a preview has been provided. Please use the Get access link above for information on how to access this content.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{gutting1989archaeology,
    author = "Gutting, Gary",
    title = "Michel Foucault's Archaeology of Scientific Reason",
    year = "1989",
    doi = "10.1017/cbo9781139172141",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Gutting undertakes a systematic reconstruction of Foucault's archaeological method, providing a lucid exposition of the "archaeological" approach to the history of thought as a method for uncovering the "unconscious" structures that set boundaries on the thinking of a given epoch. Through detailed analyses of History of Madness, The Birth of the Clinic, The Order of Things, and The Archaeology of Knowledge, the book relates Foucault's work to two previously neglected influences, Gaston Bachelard's philosophy of science and Georges Canguilhem's history of science, yielding an account of Foucault as engaged with the history and philosophy of science that balances the more common view of him as primarily a social critic and theorist. RELEVANCE: As the first sustained monograph-length defense of archaeology's internal logic as a distinctive form of historical explanation, this book supplies the interpretive baseline against which later analytic readings of archaeology (Tiisala, Krarup, Peters — Domain 1) implicitly react, and it directly addresses this domain's question of what kind of method archaeology is and how it stands in relation to prior history-of-science traditions. POSITION: Foundational secondary interpretation situating archaeology within the Bachelard-Canguilhem historical-epistemology tradition rather than treating it as a form of structuralism or a mere precursor to genealogy.},
    keywords = "archaeology, historical-epistemology, scientific-reason, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "This book is an important introduction to the critical interpretation of the work of the major French thinker Michel Foucault. Through comprehensive and detailed analyses of such important texts as The History of Madness in the Age of Reason, The Birth of the Clinic, The Order of Things, and The Archaeology of Knowledge, Professor Gutting provides a lucid exposition of Foucault's 'archaeological' approach to the history of thought - a method for uncovering the 'unconscious' structures that set boundaries on the thinking of a given epoch. The book also casts Foucault in a new light, relating his work to two major but neglected influences: Gaston Bachelard's philosophy of science and Georges Canguilhem's history of science. This perspective yields a new and valuable understanding of science, balancing and complementing the more common view that he was primarily a social critic and theorist. An excellent guide for those first approaching Foucault's work, the book will also be a challenging interpretation and evaluation for those already familiar with his writings.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{wartenberg2017archaeological,
    author = "Wartenberg, Thomas E.",
    title = "Foucault's Archaeological Method: A Response to Hacking and Rorty",
    year = "2017",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Wartenberg's essay engages the influential critical assessments of Foucault's archaeological method offered by Ian Hacking and Richard Rorty. It characterizes Hacking's explicit purpose as forcing a reluctant Anglo-American audience to take Foucault seriously as a philosopher, on the grounds that analytic philosophers have much to learn about the structure of certain fields of knowledge from Foucault's work, even as Hacking's strategy for making this work "palatable" is to locate a place for it on the existing analytic philosophic landscape; Wartenberg then offers his own response to this framing and to Rorty's related assessment. RELEVANCE: Because this piece explicates and responds to Hacking's widely cited essay (also included in this domain), the pairing of the two texts lets synthesis readers reconstruct one of the earliest sustained analytic disputes over whether Foucauldian archaeology can bear the explanatory weight Foucault assigns it — directly bearing on this domain's question of archaeology's claims about historical order and rupture, and on whether analytic categories are adequate to characterize it. POSITION: Engages and responds to Anglo-American analytic assessments (Hacking, Rorty) of whether and how Foucauldian archaeology can be assimilated to existing analytic philosophical categories.},
    keywords = "archaeology, Hacking, Rorty, defense, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, doi, publisher, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "Over the last twenty years, Michel Foucault has produced a body of historical-philosophical work of great significance. In a series of remarkable books, he has undertaken a bold reinterpretation of Western Society since the Renaissance. As a result of its sweeping scope and penetrating analyses, his work has been the focus of a great deal of attention and controversy. It is Hacking’s explicit purpose to force a somewhat unwilling Anglo-American audience to take Foucault seriously as a philosopher. Hacking feels that analytic philosophers have a good deal to learn about the structure of certain fields of knowledge from Foucault, but he also recognizes that there is much in Foucault’s work that would put them off. Hacking’s strategy, then, for making Foucault’s work palatable to analytic philosophers is to find a place on the analytic philosophic landscape for it to occupy.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{hacking1986archaeology,
    author = "Hacking, Ian",
    editor = "Hoy, David Couzens",
    title = "The Archaeology of Foucault",
    year = "1986",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: This essay offers one of the earliest and most influential Anglo-American analytic engagements with Foucault's archaeological method, examining Foucault's account of discursive "positivities" and historical rule-structures; its stature as a target of direct philosophical response (see Wartenberg, this domain) attests to its role in setting the terms of subsequent analytic debate over whether archaeology constitutes a defensible mode of historical explanation. RELEVANCE: As the essay to which Wartenberg's paper is an explicit rebuttal, this piece is a necessary reference point for reconstructing the analytic reception of archaeology and for assessing this domain's question of whether archaeology's claims about historical rupture and "conditions of possibility" withstand philosophical scrutiny. POSITION: Early, influential critical assessment of Foucauldian archaeology from within Anglo-American philosophy of science and language, cited across the subsequent secondary literature (Koopman, Elden, and others) as a reference point for evaluating archaeology's coherence.},
    keywords = "archaeology, positivities, critical-reception, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, publisher, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@misc{webb2012archaeology,
    author = "Webb, David",
    title = "Foucault's Archaeology: Science and Transformation",
    year = "2012",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Webb argues that Foucault's archaeology is best understood as a direct response to the predicament for thought in modernity that Foucault describes in the closing chapters of The Order of Things, and that mathematics and science are fundamental to the possibility of this response. He situates Foucault's conception of discourse, and above all the "historical a priori," against the background of what Foucault calls the "mathematical a priori," and provides an analysis of "temporal dispersion" tracing this idea back to Foucault's critique of Kant, culminating in a detailed commentary on The Archaeology of Knowledge. RELEVANCE: By grounding the historical a priori in Foucault's engagement with mathematics and the Kantian a priori rather than solely in the Husserlian lineage emphasized elsewhere in this domain (Carr, Moran, Thompson), this book offers an alternative genealogy of Foucault's central archaeological concept and bears directly on this domain's question of what "conditions of possibility" means for Foucault. POSITION: Reads archaeology as continuous with, and answerable to, the philosophy of mathematics and science (via the "mathematical a priori"), complementing but diverging from the Husserl-centered accounts of the historical a priori.},
    keywords = "archaeology, historical-a-priori, mathematics, Kant, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: doi, publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "This book provides a new perspective on Foucault’s The Archaeology of Knowledge by revealing the extent to which its approach to language was influenced by the mathematical sciences. Setting out this background to Foucault’s analysis makes The Archaeology of Knowledge both accessible in a new way, and relevant to issues that are at the heart of much contemporary debate over the nature of critical thought and the relation between philosophy and the sciences. This book sheds new light on a crucial period of Foucault’s work by highlighting his relation to thinkers such as Cavaillès and Serres. It aims to provide a reading of The Archaeology of Knowledge that puts it at the heart Foucault’s thought. Rather than attempting a scientific study of language as such, Foucault is shown to have adopted a mode of thought indebted to thinkers in the scientific and epistemological tradition",
    abstract_source = "core"
}

@misc{carr2016husserl,
    author = "Carr, David",
    title = "Husserl and Foucault on the historical apriori: teleological and anti-teleological views of history",
    year = "2016",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Carr examines why Husserl and Foucault both employ the striking phrase "the historical a priori" at key points in their work despite meaning very different things by it, arguing that the difference tracks a deeper disagreement over whether history has an intrinsic teleological structure (Husserl) or must be understood in explicitly anti-teleological, discontinuist terms (Foucault). RELEVANCE: This paper directly addresses this domain's question of how archaeology positions itself against alternative (in this case teleological/phenomenological) models of historical order, using the historical a priori as the test case for that opposition. POSITION: Comparative analysis contrasting Husserl's teleological and Foucault's anti-teleological uses of "the historical a priori," part of a cluster of 2016 Continental Philosophy Review papers on this question.},
    keywords = "historical-a-priori, Husserl, teleology, archaeology, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@article{allen2017psychoanalysis,
    author = "Allen, Amy",
    title = {"Psychoanalysis and Ethnology" Revisited: Foucault's Historicization of History},
    journal = "The Southern Journal of Philosophy",
    year = "2017",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Allen re-examines the closing sections of The Order of Things to address the longstanding question of whether Foucault is best understood as a philosopher or a historian, arguing that this framing misses the point of Foucault's project, which is to historicize the very notion of "history" itself — a notion Foucault takes to be central to the historical a priori of modernity. RELEVANCE: By arguing that Foucault is neither simply a philosopher (because he treats philosophy in modernity as a historical enterprise) nor simply a historian (because his historical approach aims to transform the modern historical a priori from within), this paper speaks directly to this domain's question of how archaeology positions itself relative to standard historiographical models, reframing the question itself as symptomatic of the very historical a priori Foucault is diagnosing. POSITION: Argues that the philosopher/historian dichotomy is itself an artifact of the modern historical a priori that Foucault's archaeology sets out to historicize, rather than a well-formed question about Foucault's disciplinary identity.},
    keywords = "historical-a-priori, historicism, Order-of-Things, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi",
    abstract = "Abstract This article re‐examines the closing sections of Michel Foucault's The Order of Things in order to address the longstanding question of whether he is best understood as a philosopher or a historian. My central argument is that this question misses the crucial point of Foucault's work, which is to historicize the notion of history (as it is traditionally understood, represented by History with a capital H), which Foucault takes to be central to the historical a priori of modernity. An examination of his historicization of History thus reveals that Foucault is neither simply a philosopher—because he conceives of philosophy in modernity as a historical enterprise—nor a historian—because his own historical approach is designed to transform the modern historical a priori from within. This analysis also sheds new light of Foucault's relationship to psychoanalysis and his conception of critique.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{basso2012historicity,
    author = "Basso, Elisabetta",
    title = "On Historicity and Transcendentality Again. Foucault's Trajectory from Existential Psychiatry to Historical Epistemology",
    journal = "Foucault Studies",
    year = "2012",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Basso traces the emergence of the concept of the "historical a priori" back to the young Foucault's engagement with existential psychiatry, focusing on the French philosophical context of Foucault's 1954 introduction to Ludwig Binswanger's "Dream and Existence." She argues that this early "phenomenological" phase of Foucault's work is methodologically coherent with the later development of archaeology as "historical epistemology," and that archaeology is methodologically linked to Canguilhem's epistemology insofar as the latter also attempts to link historicity and transcendentality. RELEVANCE: This paper supplies the developmental/biographical backstory for the historical-a-priori debate addressed by Carr, Moran, Thompson, and Aldea \& Allen in this domain, showing that the concept has roots predating The Order of Things in Foucault's phenomenological and psychiatric engagements, and directly connects archaeology to the "historical epistemology" tradition central to this domain's key questions. POSITION: Continuity thesis linking Foucault's early phenomenological/psychiatric period to archaeology via the shared problem of historicity and transcendentality, situating archaeology within Canguilhemian historical epistemology.},
    keywords = "historical-a-priori, historical-epistemology, Canguilhem, phenomenology, High, doi-verified, METADATA\_CLEANED: pages, doi",
    abstract = "In this paper I focus on the emergence of the concept of the “historical a priori” at the origin of Foucault’s archeology. I emphasize the methodological function of this concept within Foucault’s archaeology, and I maintain that despite the different thesis it entails as compared to its philosophical sources, it pertains to one of the main issues of phenomenology, that is, the problematization of the relation between reality as it appears in its historicity, and transcendentality. I start from the interest of the young Foucault in existential psychiatry, and I focus on the French philosophical context in which Foucault’s Introduction to Ludwig Binswanger’s “Dream and Existence” (1954) was conceived. My aim is to show that the first “phenomenological” phase of Foucault’s work is coherent, from a methodological point of view, with the development of archaeology intended as “historical epistemology.” I conclude by arguing that Foucault’s archaeology is methodologically linked to Canguilhem’s epistemology, in that the latter presents itself as an important attempt at linking together historicity and transcendentality.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{thompson2016historical,
    author = "Thompson, Kevin",
    title = "From the historical a priori to the dispositif: Foucault, the phenomenological legacy, and the problem of transcendental genesis",
    year = "2016",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Thompson asks what philosophical motivations lay behind the emergence of the genealogical method in Foucault's thought, and argues — against traditional interpretations that treat genealogy as a replacement for archaeology — that genealogy is best construed as a supplementary addition to the archaeological mode of investigation, tracing the movement from the "historical a priori" to the later concept of the "dispositif" through the problem of transcendental genesis inherited from the phenomenological tradition. RELEVANCE: This paper bears directly on this domain's core question of whether the archaeology-to-genealogy transition is a break, a supplement, or a continuous development, arguing explicitly for the supplementation reading via a close analysis of how the "dispositif" concept absorbs and extends the "historical a priori." POSITION: Supplementation thesis — genealogy extends rather than supersedes archaeology, resolving the problem of transcendental genesis that the historical a priori alone could not.},
    keywords = "historical-a-priori, dispositif, archaeology-genealogy-transition, phenomenology, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{koopman2013genealogy,
    author = "Koopman, Colin",
    title = "Genealogy as Critique: Foucault and the Problems of Modernity",
    year = "2013",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Koopman reads Foucault's genealogical method in light of a wider tradition of Continental and American philosophy — Nietzsche, Habermas, Deleuze, Rorty, Bernard Williams, and Ian Hacking — arguing that philosophical genealogy involves not only the critique of modernity but also its practical transformation, and developing genealogy as both a philosophical tradition and a method for understanding the complex histories of present social and cultural conditions ("problematizations"). RELEVANCE: As a monograph-length treatment of genealogy specifically as a philosophical method (rather than a topical application of Foucault), this book is a central secondary reference for this domain's question of what genealogy is as a mode of history, complementing Koopman's 2008 article (Domain 1) on the archaeology-to-genealogy "expansion" with a fuller account of genealogy's critical function. POSITION: Develops genealogy as a distinctively transformative (not merely diagnostic) critical-historical method, situating Foucault within a broader genealogical tradition running through Nietzsche, Williams, and Hacking.},
    keywords = "genealogy, critique, problematization, modernity, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: doi, publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = {The overall aim of Colin Koopman'sGenealogy as Critiqueis "to explicate genealogy in such a way as to show that it offers a valuable, effective, and uniquely important practice of philosophical-historical critique of the present" (5). Michel Foucault's genealogical method, which serves as Koopman's paradigm case of genealogy, is enormously influential but often misunderstood by critics and fans alike. Koopman's defense of genealogy rests on a two-step revision of our understanding of Foucault's method: first, Koopman rethinks the relationship between Foucaultian genealogy and Kantian critique; second, he interprets Foucault's practice of Kantian critique through the lens of problematization. Once we reinterpret Foucaultian genealogy along these lines, Koopman argues, we will be able to see that his work belongs in conversation with that of critical theorists such as Habermas and pragmatists such as Dewey and Rorty, rather than with the Continental high theorists -- Derrida, Lacan, and Agamben -- with whom he is more often associated. In the end, Koopman proposes an ambitious methodological reconciliation of Foucaultian genealogy with pragmatist critical theory in which the former fulfills the backward looking, diagnostic task of articulating our most pressing problems and the latter fulfills the forward looking, anticipatory task of suggesting possible responses to those problems. The book divides into roughly three, not entirely equal, parts. After an introductory chapter that situates Koopman's view within existing Foucault scholarship, the first four chapters explicate the method of genealogical problematization, understood as a transformative renewal of the Kantian notion of critique. The next two chapters re-read Foucault's work in light of the account of his method offered in the first half of the book. The concluding chapter makes the case for the methodological reconciliation of Foucaultian genealogy as problematization and pragmatist critical theory. The book as a whole is guided by Koopman's understanding of philosophy as a critical enterprise, "an immanent and reflexive engagement with the full complexity and contingency of the conditions of possibility for doing, being, and thinking in our cultural present" (23). Chapter one introduces the concept of problematization and argues that it is the key to understanding Foucault's method and thus hisoeuvre. Although the term "problematization" only appears with regularity and in Koopman's precise sense in Foucault's late work, Koopman argues quite compellingly that "the concept of problematization provides us with a better grip on the full range of Foucault's methodology than do episteme, discursive formation,dispositif, and other such concepts," all of which are best understood as "successive attempts to work out the kind of idea that achieves self-clarity with the concept of problematization" (53). In the course of developing this argument, Koopman offers a novel account of the relationship between archaeology and genealogy whereby the latter is understood as an expansion of rather than a replacement for the former. Whereas archaeology focused on temporal discontinuity, genealogy expanded this focus and embraced both continuity and discontinuity in history; whereas archaeology focused on knowledge, genealogy expanded this focus to power-knowledge relations. As Koopman puts it, archaeology-plus-genealogy traverses multiple vectors (power-knowledge rather than just power) along multiple temporalities (continuous and discontinuous). With respect to the master concept of problematization, archaeology articulates existing problematizations in their static forms, whereas genealogy traces their contingent emergence in and through complex practices (47). With the focus now squarely on genealogy, chapter two zeroes in on the genealogical method. Koopman's main aim here is to distinguish Foucault's problematizing genealogical method from the subversive and vindicatory versions of genealogy offered by Nietzsche and Bernard Williams, respectively; he also argues that this characterization of Foucaultian genealogy shows why the genetic fallacy objection, often raised against Foucault, misses the mark. What distinguishes Foucault's problematizing genealogy from Nietzsche's and Williams's more normatively ambitious versions is that whereas they used genealogy to generate normative justifications -- Williams used it to vindicate our concept of truthfulness whereas Nietzsche used it to undermine Christian morality -- Foucault used it for the normatively modest task of clarifying and intensifying our problematizations. If, Koopman argues, Foucault wasn't trying to draw any normative conclusions from his genealogical arguments, then he can hardly be guilty of inappropriately drawing normative conclusions about some present concept or practice on the basis of an analysis of its history, as the genetic fallacy charge alleges. Foucault's genealogies do provide resources for normative critique, on Koopman's view, but his genealogies by themselves are not meant to be tools of normative assessment. Koopman thinks this also shows why Foucault's version of genealogy is superior to Nietzsche's and Williams's, since both Nietzsche and Williams try to get more from genealogy than it can possibly give. Chapter three explicates the core ideas of genealogy as problematization: contingency, complexity, and critique. This chapter and the next one -- titled "What Problematization Is" and "What Problematization Does" -- are really the core of the book. Here, Koopman helpfully pins down the up till now rather slippery term "problematization," noting that it has two dimensions and that each of these dimensions has two senses. A problematization is both "an act of critical inquiry . . . and a nominal object of inquiry," hence the term has both a verbal (to problematize) and a nominal (a problematization) form (98). Foucaultian genealogy engages both dimensions of the term simultaneously: it problematizes things (that is, renders some things problematic that were not previously considered as such) and it articulates problematizations (that is, things that have become problematic and the process by which they have become so). Further, each of these dimensions of problematization has two senses: to problematize is both to describe and to critique existing problematizations, and a problematization has both negative and positive aspects (100). Problematizations are both contingent and complex; hence, genealogy as problematization "tracks the complex interaction of different practical vectors in their contingent intersection with one another so as to form problematizations" (108). These problematizations form the conditions of possibility of the present, and this brings us to Koopman's account of Foucault's Kantianism. In articulating Foucault's debt to Kant, Koopman helpfully distinguishes between the general Kantian notion of critique and the much more demanding transcendental form of critique that Kant practiced in his own critical philosophy. Koopman insists that although Foucault engages in a Kantian project of critique as an inquiry into conditions of possibility, he does not attempt even a modified version of transcendental critique. Koopman also suggests -- not entirely convincingly, in my view -- that Foucault did not deny that transcendental critique was possible or desirable; he just didn't much care for it himself. Foucault was, on Koopman's reading, a much more thoroughgoing empiricist than most scholars have realized, and as such he was relatively uninterested in the deep philosophical perplexities concerning the relationship between the empirical and the transcendental that have stymied generations of German philosophers. Although he freely engaged in conditions-of-possibility talk, he always understood those conditions to be empirically conditioned all the way down. The claim that problematizations are both contingent and complex leads Koopman in chapter four to a seemingly very simple but nonetheless important and highly original insight: that the point of genealogy is not only to demonstrate that our practices or concepts or norms or forms of life are contingent and therefore could be otherwise; genealogy also aims to showhowthose practices, concepts, norms, and forms of life have been composed through complex practices. Koopman argues that this aspect of genealogy is often overlooked or underappreciated even by Foucault's ablest defenders, who frequently take genealogy to be an exercise in denaturalization full stop. In Koopman's view, this is a crucial omission since "if genealogy helps us to seehowour present was made, it also thereby equips us with some of the tools we would need for beginning the labor of remaking our future differently" (130). Koopman connects this to the thought that genealogy as problematization is neither vindicatory nor subversive but rather aims toward what he calls "responsive reconstruction" (21). Armed with this characterization of Foucault's method of genealogy as problematization, chapters five and six re-read some of Foucault's major works in this new light. Chapter five focuses on Foucault's problematization of modernity. Koopman argues that this critique has often been interpreted in terms of a Weberian logic of exclusion and domination, according to which "reason dominates madness by totally excluding it, and . . . power dominates freedom by totally incorporating it" (163), whereas Foucault endeavored to make the rather different point that modernity operates according to a futile logic of purification, according to which reason is purified of madness and freedom of power. For Koopman's Foucault, this logic is futile because reason/madness and power/freedom presuppose one another in modernity; they are, in other words, "reciprocal and incompatible" (163). Hence, the proper response to Foucault's critique is not the liberation of madness or freedom but rather a "critical-experimental practice of self-transformation" (174). Koopman argues that this contrast between freedom as liberation, which Foucault regarded as deeply problematic, and freedom as critical self-transformation, which he offered as a reconstructive response to his problematization of modernity, accounts for his apparent ambivalence about the concept of freedom.},
    abstract_source = "ndpr"
}

@misc{lichtenstein2022explanation,
    author = "Lichtenstein, Eli B.",
    title = "Explanation and evaluation in Foucault's genealogy of morality",
    year = "2022",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Lichtenstein argues that, unlike most philosophical genealogists (who typically eschew historiographic methodology in favor of a purely philosophical approach), Foucault's early-1970s genealogical method borrows from contemporary historiographic models of explanation, expanding the range of objects proper to genealogical accounts of historical change by modifying two central Nietzschean commitments. The paper further argues that historical method has normative relevance for genealogy, since different historiographic choices lead to different normative conclusions, expanding both the range of objects subject to evaluative assessment and the prescriptive recommendations that follow. RELEVANCE: This paper speaks precisely to this domain's question of how genealogy positions itself against causal historiographic models of explanation, arguing that Foucauldian genealogy does not simply reject historiographic explanation but rather appropriates and expands it for critical-evaluative ends — directly bearing on the causal/evaluative distinction central to this domain. POSITION: Argues that Foucauldian genealogy integrates historiographic explanation with evaluative critique, against readings that treat genealogy as either purely explanatory or purely evaluative.",
    keywords = "genealogy, explanation, causal-history, normativity, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Abstract Philosophers have cataloged a range of genealogical methods by which different sorts of normative conclusions can be established. Although such methods provide diverging ways of pursuing genealogical inquiry, they typically converge in eschewing historiographic methodology, in favor of a uniquely philosophical approach. In contrast, one genealogist who drew on historiographic methodology is Michel Foucault. This article presents the motivations and advantages of Foucault's genealogical use of such a methodology. It advances two mains claims. First, that Foucault's early 1970s work employs a distinct genealogical method, which borrows from contemporary historiographic models of explanation to expand the range of objects that are proper to genealogical accounts of historical change. I demonstrate how Foucault modifies two central commitments of Nietzsche by broadening the dimensions of genealogical inquiry and explanation. Second, that historical method has normative relevance for genealogy, insofar as different historiographic choices can lead to different normative conclusions. I motivate this second claim by explaining how Foucault's multidimensional genealogical method expands both (a) the range of objects that are subject to evaluative assessment, and (b) the set of possible prescriptive recommendations that follow from such assessment.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{tjampens2020entering,
    author = "T'Jampens, Michiel and Versieren, Jelle",
    title = {Entering the Archive: "Il faut défendre la société" and Michel Foucault's Critical Archeological Inquiry into the History and Method of Genealogy},
    year = "2020",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: The authors argue that in "Society Must Be Defended," Foucault attempted to historicize and criticize Nietzsche's equation of the social with struggle by producing a descriptive discursive history of his own genealogical project — deploying, in effect, the method of "critical archaeology" upon genealogy itself. In doing so, Foucault discovered a close historical and conceptual bond between the genealogical discourse of struggle and modern racial discourses, which led him to distance his own genealogical concept of struggle from Nietzsche's, rethinking the social within the framework of gouvernementalité, in which struggle becomes a modality rather than the prime mover of society. RELEVANCE: This paper directly addresses this domain's question of the relationship between archaeology and genealogy by showing Foucault using archaeological technique to historicize genealogy's own emergence, complicating any simple narrative of a clean methodological break between the two — a case study of archaeology and genealogy operating jointly on the same material. POSITION: Shows genealogy and archaeology as methodologically intertwined (rather than sequential or discrete) in Foucault's own practice, via a close reading of the 1975-76 lectures.},
    keywords = "genealogy, archaeology, Society-Must-Be-Defended, Nietzsche, race, Medium, doi-verified, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "In “Il faut défendre la société”, Foucault attempted to historicize and criticize Nietzsche’s equating of the social with struggle. In order to do so, Foucault produced a descriptive discursive history of his genealogical project by deploying the method of the critical archaeology. Foucault realized thereinafter that his archaeological exposition of the genealogical discourse in fact laid bare a close historical and conceptual bond between genealogy and modern racial discourses. In the first lectures, Foucault, unearthed the genealogical discourse hidden in the literature written by the nobility as they attempted to resist the centralisation of royal power. In the latter part of his lectures, he described a discursive interplay between genealogy-as-struggle and the biopolitical practices of the modern state. As such, he gave a tentatively description how the modern state inherited and extensively applied the notion of struggle in its biopolitical control on its populations. The immoral and historical consequences of this affinity, resulting in the biopolitics of genocide, warranted Foucault to distance himself from Nietzsche’s concept, which in effect resulted in rethinking the social within the framework of gouvernmentalité, in which struggle was a modality rather than the prime mover of society.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{aldeaallen2016history,
    author = "Aldea, Andreea Smaranda and Allen, Amy",
    title = "History, critique, and freedom: the historical a priori in Husserl and Foucault",
    year = "2016",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: As the introduction to a themed cluster of papers on the historical a priori in Husserl and Foucault (which includes Carr, Moran, and Thompson in this domain), Aldea and Allen frame the shared philosophical stakes of using "the historical a priori" to link questions of historicity, critique, and freedom, setting up the competing teleological (Husserlian) and anti-teleological (Foucauldian) accounts of historical intelligibility debated across the cluster. RELEVANCE: This paper contextualizes and organizes the historical-a-priori debate that is central to this domain's treatment of archaeology's claims about the "conditions of possibility" of knowledge, making explicit why the comparison to Husserl matters for assessing whether Foucault's account of historical order is philosophically defensible. POSITION: Editorial/framing piece establishing the shared problematic (historicity, critique, freedom) that organizes the Husserl-Foucault historical a priori debate.},
    keywords = "historical-a-priori, Husserl, freedom, critique, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{moran2016sinnboden,
    author = "Moran, Dermot",
    title = "Sinnboden der Geschichte: Foucault and Husserl on the structural a priori of history",
    year = "2016",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Moran explores Husserl's and Foucault's respective approaches to the historical a priori and defends Husserl's account as the richer of the two, arguing that Foucault's own conception of the historical a priori is ambiguous and fails to have full explanatory value because it misunderstands the need for an a priori to be both universal and necessary, offering no adequate account of the "a priori of historicity" that, for Husserl, is essential to human cultural life. RELEVANCE: This paper provides the critical counterweight within this domain's Husserl-Foucault cluster (against Carr's and Thompson's more sympathetic readings), directly engaging this domain's question of whether archaeology's central category of historical order can be philosophically sustained on its own terms. POSITION: Defends Husserl's phenomenological account of the historical a priori as superior to and more rigorous than Foucault's, challenging the coherence of Foucault's archaeological category.},
    keywords = "historical-a-priori, Husserl, critique-of-Foucault, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{hyder2009foucault,
    author = "Hyder, David",
    title = "Foucault, Cavaillès, and Husserl on the Historical Epistemology of the Sciences",
    year = "2009",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Hyder traces the origins of two key notions in Foucault's work up to and including The Archaeology of Knowledge: the notion of "archaeology" itself as a form of historical investigation distinguished from the mere history of ideas, and Foucault's "historical a prioris." He argues that Foucault and Cavaillès both retain the general outlines of Husserl's phenomenological archaeology of the sciences (via the technique of reactivating sedimented intentional acts) while rejecting its appeal to conscious acts of meaning and "the philosophy of consciousness." RELEVANCE: This paper supplies the specific genealogical link (via Cavaillès) between Foucault's archaeology and the Husserlian phenomenological tradition of "historical epistemology," complementing Basso's account and grounding this domain's question of how archaeology positions itself against phenomenological/hermeneutic models of history. POSITION: Traces archaeology's origin to a Cavaillès-mediated critique and adaptation of Husserlian phenomenological archaeology, situating Foucault within the same historical-epistemology lineage as Bachelard and Canguilhem.},
    keywords = "archaeology, historical-epistemology, Cavailles, Husserl, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, doi, publisher, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@misc{garruzzo2025willtopower,
    author = "Garruzzo, Anthony",
    title = "History and the Will to Power: Foucault and Nietzsche on Genealogy",
    year = "2025",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Garruzzo argues that the recent revival of "genealogy" among social and political philosophers has obscured what actually distinguishes genealogy as Nietzsche and Foucault practiced it: not merely any normatively-loaded account of origins, but specifically an account of the contingent emergence of an institution, practice, or concept within a regime of power and control, understood through Nietzsche's "will to power." On this reading, genealogy's critical contribution is not normative insight but "tactical knowledge" -- historical investigation of past struggles for power illuminates strategies for exercising and resisting power in the present. RELEVANCE: This paper bears directly on how Nietzschean Foucault's genealogy actually is, arguing for a strong continuity via the will-to-power framework while distinguishing genuine genealogy from looser contemporary uses of the term, which sharpens the project's account of what Foucault takes from Nietzsche specifically. POSITION: Reconstructs Foucauldian genealogy as inheriting Nietzsche's will-to-power framework, defending a substantive rather than merely rhetorical continuity between the two thinkers.},
    keywords = "Nietzsche, genealogy, will-to-power, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "ABSTRACT In recent years, there has been a revival of interest in genealogy among social and political philosophers. I argue, however, that this growing literature has tended to obscure what distinguishes genealogy as an approach to the interpretation of history. Any normative evaluation of something that appeals to an account of its origins has come to be called a “genealogy,” even if this account is explicitly hypothetical or unabashedly teleological. But a genealogy, according to Friedrich Nietzsche and Michel Foucault, is not merely an account of origins: it is an account of the contingent origins of the integration of an institution, practice, or concept within a regime of power and control . In other words, genealogy investigates an institution, practice, or concept through the lens of what Nietzsche calls “the will to power.” This paper aims to reconstruct the conception of the will to power Foucault inherits from Nietzsche in a way that explains why investigations into struggles for power from the past can provide insight into the strategies used for exercising and maintaining power in the present. The critical contribution of genealogy, I argue, is not normative insight but tactical knowledge : to oppose something successfully, first you must know your enemy .",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{testa2025genealogyhealth,
    author = "Testa, Federico",
    title = "Genealogy Between Health and Illness: On the Ambiguity of the Historical Sense in Foucault's 1969--1970 Vincennes Lectures on Nietzsche",
    journal = "Foucault Studies",
    year = "2025",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Testa revisits Foucault's reading of Nietzsche through the lens of medical metaphor, arguing that alongside the well-known emphasis on genealogy as "diagnosis," Foucault's 1969--1970 Vincennes lectures on Nietzsche develop a less-explored notion of genealogy as a "curative art" or "science of remedies," in which the Nietzschean "historical sense" functions as both poison and cure -- an immanent critique of the present that carries the possibility of its own transfiguration. RELEVANCE: By recovering under-examined lecture material predating "Nietzsche, Genealogy, History" (1971), this paper supplies primary evidence for how Foucault's engagement with Nietzsche developed prior to his canonical published statement on genealogy, refining claims about continuity or departure between the two thinkers' philosophies of history. POSITION: Close archival/textual reading emphasizing the medical-therapeutic dimension of Foucault's Nietzscheanism, developed from unpublished/lesser-known lecture material rather than the published essay alone.},
    keywords = "Nietzsche, genealogy, historical-sense, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, pages, doi",
    abstract = {ABSTRACT: This article revisits Foucault's reading of Nietzsche, with a special focus on the medical metaphors surrounding the definition and practice of genealogy. It argues that, in addition to the emphasis on the notion of diagnosis as a key medically inspired element of genealogy, it is important to consider an aspect that has been significantly less explored in existing scholarship on Foucault's appropriation of Nietzsche's philosophy: that of genealogy as a "curative" art or as a "science of remedies." Hence the article presents Nietzsche as a thinker of health and disease, and his views on the philosopher as a kind of physician. Then, it moves on to examine the "historical sense" as a notion that plays a key role in Foucault's reading of Nietzsche in his 1969–1970 lectures at Vincennes and his definition of genealogy as a practice of history that both emerges from epochal disease and points to possible forms of healing. My claim is that, in the Vincennes lectures on Nietzsche, the historical sense can be described as something that can poison and heal, and as a process of immanent critique of the present, carrying the possibility of its transfiguration.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{rockhill2020genealogycounterhistory,
    author = "Rockhill, Gabriel",
    title = "Foucault, Genealogy, Counter-History",
    year = "2020",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Rockhill examines the force and limits of Foucauldian genealogy in order to develop an alternative practice of "counter-history." Centering on Foucault's essay on Nietzsche, he elucidates Foucault's symptomatological distinction between two normatively opposed conceptions of origin in Nietzsche -- Herkunft (descent) and Ursprung (origin) -- and demonstrates that this supposedly "properly Nietzschean" textual distinction does not actually exist in Nietzsche's text as Foucault presents it, revealing Foucault's appropriation of Nietzsche to be a strategic invention. He then proposes an alternative, multi-dimensional "counter-history" that dispenses with genealogy's residual reliance on moralized narratives of origin. RELEVANCE: This is a sustained critical (not merely expository) treatment of exactly how faithful Foucault's genealogy is to Nietzsche, directly answering the project's question of where Foucault departs from Nietzsche by showing the departure occurs at the textual and conceptual foundations of the 1971 essay itself. POSITION: Critical of the standard view that Foucauldian genealogy simply extends Nietzsche; argues the Herkunft/Ursprung distinction central to Foucault's Nietzscheanism is Foucault's own construction, and proposes moving beyond genealogy toward "counter-history."},
    keywords = "Nietzsche, genealogy, Herkunft, Ursprung, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = {Abstract:This article examines the force and limitations of genealogy in order to develop a practice of counter-history that is capable of both overcoming its inherent problems and providing a more trenchant mode of critico-historical engagement. Using Foucault's well-known essay on Nietzsche as its methodological centerpiece, it begins by elucidating the latter's powerful contribution to the historical analysis of values, while also foregrounding the quasi-naturalized morality of genealogy that structures it. Against this backdrop, it examines Foucault's symptomatological distinction between two opposed and normativized conceptions of origin in Nietzsche—Herkunft and Ursprung—in order to both explicate Foucault's unique appropriation of Nietzschean genealogy and demonstrate its limits through the striking fact that this originary textual symptom of "properly Nietzschean" genealogy does not actually exist in the text. The remainder of the article draws on certain genealogical resources while challenging the historical order undergirding them in order to propose an alternative logic of history that takes into account its constitutive multidimensionality and the multiplicity of agencies at work in any conjuncture. It dismantles, in this way, the very framework that renders historical origins possible, as well as streamlined moral narratives of genealogical inversion, thereby parting ways with the moralities of genealogy in favor of the politicization of values.},
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{elden2025dynastics,
    author = "Elden, Stuart",
    title = "Foucault, Dynastics and Power Relations: Between Archaeology and Genealogy",
    year = "2025",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Elden traces Foucault's use of the term "dynastics" (from dunamis/dunasteia) in lectures and one interview from the early 1970s -- after Foucault's explicit critical engagement with Nietzsche in "Nietzsche, Genealogy, History" (1971) but before he settled on "genealogy" as his term of art -- as a complementary approach to archaeology relating to both power and knowledge. He argues that even after Foucault drops the term, "dynastics" continues to inform how Foucault subsequently writes about power relations, dynamics, and lineage. RELEVANCE: This paper provides fine-grained conceptual-historical evidence for exactly how and when Foucault's Nietzschean turn reshaped his terminology for historical method, refining the standard "archaeology-to-genealogy" narrative with an intermediate, Nietzsche-adjacent stage the project can draw on to periodize Foucault's methodological development. POSITION: Philological/conceptual-historical study establishing an under-recognized transitional vocabulary (dynastics) between archaeology and genealogy, tied to but distinct from Foucault's explicit Nietzschean turn.},
    keywords = "Nietzsche, archaeology, genealogy, dynastics, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Michel Foucault's historical approach is usually understood as moving from archaeology to genealogy, the former describing his work of the 1960s and the latter the 1970s. From the mid-1970s Foucault certainly describes his work as genealogy, and he explicitly relates this to Nietzsche's project, which he had critically explored in lectures and the famous ‘Nietzsche, Genealogy, History’ essay published in 1971. But in lectures and one interview from the early 1970s, Foucault uses another term to describe the complementary approach to archaeology, which is that of dynastics, a mode of analysis he relates to both power and knowledge. Tracing the usage of these terms over time, this article explores their relation. Foucault uses the term dynastics to describe his approach after his explicit engagement with Nietzsche, and before settling on genealogy as the appropriate term. Foucault's use of dynastics is interesting for many reasons, including the way he glosses this as dunamis dunasteia. Foucault is here thinking about a range of senses, from dynamics or power to dynasties, heredities, and lineage. Even after he drops the term to describe his approach, Foucault is perhaps invoking the notion of dynastics every time he subsequently writes about power relations.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{karlsenvilladsen2018maoism,
    author = "Karlsen, Mads Peter and Villadsen, Kaspar",
    title = "Foucault -- maoismen, genealogien",
    year = "2018",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Against the near-universal emphasis on Nietzsche as the source of Foucault's genealogical method, Karlsen and Villadsen argue that Foucault's practical involvement in the Maoist-inspired activist group Groupe d'information sur les prisons (GIP) left crucial marks on his contemporaneous statements on genealogy and the power-knowledge nexus, and that this activism is directly reflected in his 1976 Collège de France lectures. They argue this yields a more nuanced picture of Foucault's relationship to Marxism than the standard "unambiguously negative" portrayal. RELEVANCE: This paper directly complicates the project's Nietzsche-genealogy question by identifying a second, largely neglected dialogue partner (French Maoism/Marxism) for the genealogical method, and simultaneously supplies primary material for the project's Marxism cluster by showing genealogy's practical entanglement with Marxist-activist politics rather than only theoretical debate. POSITION: Revisionist account locating genealogy's sources jointly in Nietzsche and in Foucault's Maoist political practice, complicating both the "faithful Nietzschean" and "anti-Marxist" standard narratives.},
    keywords = "Nietzsche, Marxism, Maoism, genealogy, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Foucault’s inspiration from Nietzsche in terms of his approach to the writing of history is difficult to overestimate. However, this article will advance an interpretation of Foucault’s approach to history which focuses on another, and less readily evident, dialogue partner in his authorship, namely the Marxist tradition and, more precisely, French Maoism. In the first part it is argued that Foucault’s practical experience from his involvement in the Maoist inspired activist group, Groupe d’information sur les prisons (GIP), left crucial marks on his contemporaneous statements on the genealogical method and his elaboration of the power-knowledge nexus. In the second part of the article it is demonstrated how the activism of GIP is reflected in his lectures at College de France in 1976. The aim of the article is threefold. Firstly, to bring attention to other (largely neglected) sources of inspiration for Foucault’s genealogical approach than Nietzsche. Secondly, to contribute to a more nuanced understanding of Foucault’s relationship to Marxism, which is frequently portrayed rather one-sidedly as unambiguously negative. And thirdly, to demonstrate concretely how principles originating from Maoist political activism reappear, not only in Foucault’s practical commitment to GIP, but also in his theoretical considerationsof genealogy.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{giuliani2021dialecticsmadness,
    author = "Giuliani, Alice",
    title = "Dialectics of Madness: Foucault, Hegel, and the Opening of the Speculative",
    year = "2021",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Giuliani argues for a substantive continuity between Hegel's speculative philosophy in the Phenomenology of Spirit and Foucault's archaeological/genealogical approach in History of Madness (1961): Foucault's configuration of madness across the classical and modern age traces a path toward self-awareness for Western rationality, and dialectical mediation operates in his account of the encounter between poverty and unreason in the hôpital and in Philippe Pinel's asile, which can be read as figures of dialectical movement. She suggests further continuities in how "difference" and "discontinuity" acquire enhanced meaning in a speculative context, while also gesturing beyond this continuity toward what Hegel's speculative opening implies past the dynamic of objectification. RELEVANCE: This chapter directly engages the project's question of Foucault's relation to Hegel and the dialectic by locating dialectical/speculative structure inside Foucault's own first major work, complicating any simple "anti-Hegelian" characterization of Foucault's earliest historical method. POSITION: Continuity thesis reading History of Madness as retaining a speculative-dialectical structure inherited from Hegel, against readings that treat Foucault's archaeology as straightforwardly anti-Hegelian or anti-dialectical from the outset.},
    keywords = "Hegel, dialectic, madness, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, publisher, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = {: This paper aims to offer a preliminary account of the continuity between Hegel's speculative philosophy in the Phenomenology of Spirit (1807) and Foucault's archaeological and genealogical approach in the Histoire de la folie (1961). We seem to find a speculative dynamic in Foucault's work, since his configuration of madness in the classical and modern age mark a path leading to self-awareness for Western rationality. Moreover, dialectical mediation seems at play in Foucault's articulation of discourse: the encounter between poverty and unreason in the Hôpital and in Philippe Pinel's asile can be considered as figures of dialectical movement. Further elements of continuity could be argued for based on the ways that "difference" and "discontinuity" take on enhanced meanings in the speculative context. The paper concludes by gesturing past continuity to outline some implications of how Hegel's speculative opening looks beyond the dynamic of objectification.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{penaguzman2020frenchhistepist,
    author = "Peña-Guzmán, David M.",
    title = "French Historical Epistemology: Discourse, Concepts, and the Norms of Rationality",
    year = "2020",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Peña-Guzmán offers an interpretation of the French historical epistemology tradition (Jean Cavaillès, Gaston Bachelard, Georges Canguilhem, Michel Foucault), arguing that despite remaining largely unread by mainstream analytic philosophers of science, this tradition is unified by two central claims: that scientific discourse (rather than scientific theories or practices per se) is the proper object of epistemology, and that scientific concepts are the fundamental building blocks of that discourse. RELEVANCE: This paper supplies the overarching interpretive framework for situating Foucault within French historical epistemology as a distinctive tradition, directly addressing the project's question of how Canguilhem and Bachelard inform Foucault's historical method by identifying the shared discourse/concept-centered features that carry over into Foucault's archaeology. POSITION: Synthetic/interpretive account establishing French historical epistemology (including Foucault) as a coherent tradition organized around discourse and concepts rather than theories, positioned as a corrective to analytic philosophy of science's neglect of this lineage.",
    keywords = "Canguilhem, Bachelard, historical-epistemology, discourse, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "In recent years, analytic philosophers have begun to recognize the value of the French school of historical epistemology (as embodied by figures such as Jean Cavaillès, Gaston Bachelard, Georges Canguilhem, and Michel Foucault) for contemporary debates in the history and philosophy of science. This tradition, which some characterize as a 'French' approach to the philosophy of science, however, remains largely un-read by mainstream philosophers of science. This article offers an interpretation of this tradition, highlighting what the author takes to be its two central features: (i) its claim that scientific discourse is the object of epistemology and (ii) its claim that scientific concepts are the building blocks of scientific discourse.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{chimisso2003tribunal,
    author = "Chimisso, Cristina",
    title = "The Tribunal of Philosophy and its Norms: History and Philosophy in Georges Canguilhem's Historical Epistemology",
    year = "2003",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Chimisso assesses Canguilhem's historical epistemology with both theoretical and historical questions in view, focusing on the relation between history and philosophy and the external philosophical norms embedded in Canguilhem's practice of "normative history." She analyzes the nature and provenance of the norm employed in this normative history, comparing it to Canguilhem's discussion of the normal and the pathological, and concludes that while critical of Canguilhem's treatment of history, his method of analyzing scientific concepts "from below" offers a useful and generalizable model for history and philosophy of science, including of philosophical concepts. RELEVANCE: This paper provides a detailed, critical account of the specific historiographical method (normative history of concepts "from below") that Canguilhem bequeaths to the French historical epistemology tradition, giving the project precise terms for comparing Canguilhem's normative history to Foucault's explicitly anti-normative discontinuist archaeology. POSITION: Critical assessment of Canguilhem's normative history of science, distinguishing it from purely descriptive historiography and identifying the external philosophical commitments it presupposes.},
    keywords = "Canguilhem, historical-epistemology, normative-history, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Abstract In this article I assess Georges Canguilhem’s historical epistemology with both theoretical and historical questions in mind. From a theoretical point of view, I am concerned with the relation between history and philosophy, and in particular with the philosophical assumptions and external norms that are involved in history writing. Moreover, I am concerned with the role that history can play in the understanding and evaluation of philosophical concepts. From a historical point of view, I regard historical epistemology, as developed by Gaston Bachelard and Georges Canguilhem, as a conception and practice which came out of the project, elaborated in France from the 1920s to the 1940s, of combining history of science and philosophy. I analyse in particular Canguilhem’s epistemology in his theory and practice of history of science. What he called ‘normative history’ is the focus of my analysis. I evaluate the question of the nature and provenience of the norm employed in normative history, and I compare it with the norm as discussed by Canguilhem in Le normal et le pathologique . While I am critical of Canguilhem’s treatment of history, I conclude that his philosophical suggestion to analyse the formation of scientific concepts ‘from below’ represents a useful model for history and philosophy of science, and that it can be very profitably extended to philosophical concepts.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{castelligattinara2018relationship,
    author = "Castelli Gattinara, Enrico",
    title = "The Relationship between History and Epistemology in Georges Canguilhem and Gaston Bachelard",
    year = "2018",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Castelli Gattinara compares Bachelard's and Canguilhem's respective uses of the history of science for epistemology, showing that Canguilhem radicalizes Bachelard by recognizing the historicity of every aspect of scientific knowledge, including its errors, and by emphasizing "ideology" and the conceptual essence recognized within the history of science. He argues the link between history and epistemology in this tradition is one of mutual influence rather than subjection, opening onto a pluralist conception of history and time that, he explicitly notes, "opens the way to the researches of Foucault and [Michel] Serres." RELEVANCE: This paper directly traces the line of inheritance the project needs from Bachelard through Canguilhem to Foucault, explaining in what specific sense Foucault's archaeology of discontinuity and historicized "discourse" (rather than ideal objects) continues the Bachelard-Canguilhem program. POSITION: Genealogical/comparative account of the Bachelard-Canguilhem relationship that explicitly identifies Foucault (and Serres) as continuators of Canguilhem's radicalized, pluralist historicization of scientific knowledge.},
    keywords = "Canguilhem, Bachelard, historical-epistemology, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "The article shows the strategic analogies, but also the differences between Bachelard and Canguilhem on the use of the history of science for epistemology. It emphasizes the importance of the ideology for Canguilhem, and the conceptual essence he recognizes in the history of science, which is read in its internal specific differences and in its complex articulations with life and reality. No concept, in fact, comes from nothing. The link between history and epistemology is not however of subjection, but of mutual influence. Canguilhem radicalizes the thought of Bachelard, and recognizes the historicity of every aspect of scientific knowledge, even of its less valued features and above all of errors. All aspects of Science are historical. The object of the history of science is not the object of the sciences, because it is always a discourse. This is why the history of science is inevitably linked to other forms of history. This opens up a pluralist conception of History and of Time, thinking of the sciences in their real body and no longer ideal or legal. Thus Canguilhem opens the way to the researches of Foucault and Serres.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{dcruz2024naturalism,
    author = "D'Cruz, Leonard",
    title = "Foucault's Naturalism: The Importance of Scientific Epistemology for the Genealogical Method",
    year = "2024",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: D'Cruz offers a novel reconstruction of Foucault's methodology emphasizing his respect for the natural sciences, arguing against readings that take Foucault to reduce knowledge to power and thereby collapse into relativism. He develops two claims: that Foucault's normative epistemological judgments (especially about the human sciences) depend on his respect for the natural sciences as a comparative standard, and that the descriptive rigor of the genealogical method derives from its being modeled on empirical inquiry. RELEVANCE: This paper directly engages the project's question of how Canguilhem/Bachelard-style historical epistemology of science informs Foucault's method, arguing that Foucault's genealogy retains a substantive naturalistic commitment inherited from that scientific-epistemological tradition rather than dispensing with it. POSITION: Revisionist naturalist reading of Foucault's genealogy, countering relativist interpretations by grounding genealogy's rigor and Foucault's normative judgments in his continued respect for -- and modeling on -- natural-scientific inquiry, in a lineage traceable to Canguilhem.",
    keywords = "Canguilhem, naturalism, genealogy, scientific-epistemology, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "This article offers a novel reconstruction of Foucault’s methodology that emphasises his respect for the natural sciences. Foucault’s work has long been suspected of reducing knowledge to power, and thus collapsing into unconstrained relativism and methodological incoherence. These concerns are predicated on a misunderstanding of Foucault’s overall approach, which takes the form of a historico-critical project rather than a normative epistemology. However, Foucault does sometimes make normative epistemological judgements, especially about the human sciences. Furthermore, there are outstanding questions about what secures the descriptive rigour of the genealogical method. To address these issues, I develop two claims that will significantly enrich our understanding of Foucault’s methodology. The first is that Foucault’s respect for the natural sciences is crucial in making sense of his normative epistemological judgements. The second is that the descriptive rigour of his genealogical method derives from the fact it is modelled on empirical inquiry.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{gurkan2017braudelfoucault,
    author = "Gürkan, Ceyhun",
    title = "Braudel and Foucault on Structure and Event: Towards a New Approach to (Neo)Liberalism and Capitalism",
    year = "2017",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Gürkan lays out the direct dialogue between Braudel and Foucault -- who reference one another in their own texts -- and comparatively evaluates their treatments of structure and event, arguing that these are best grasped through each thinker's own analytical apparatus: Braudel's tripartite schema of economic civilization for the history of capitalism, and Foucault's analytics of power and government for the history of (neo)liberalism. The paper then works toward integrating the two analytics for a fuller historical-sociological account of capitalism and neoliberalism. RELEVANCE: As one of the few pieces treating the Braudel-Foucault relationship as a direct textual dialogue rather than a loose family resemblance, this paper gives the project concrete material for assessing how Annales-style structural/serial history (via Braudel's longue durée) relates to and differs from Foucault's own structure/event distinctions in his genealogies of power. POSITION: Comparative-integrative account treating Braudel and Foucault as complementary rather than opposed with respect to structure and event, building toward a combined historical-sociological framework for capitalism and neoliberalism.",
    keywords = "Annales, Braudel, structure-event, longue-duree, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Fernand Braudel and Michel Foucault are two renowned French historians exerting enormous influence on studies of the history of capitalism and (neo)liberalism in the context of historical sociology and governmentality respectively. Inspired by their revolutionary works on history, sociology and politics, this paper first seeks to lay out the dialogue between Braudel and Foucault in the context of their references to one another in their texts and, in turn, evaluates their views on structures and events comparatively. In what follows, we claim that their ways of understanding of structures and events are best grasped around their own analytics: Braudel's tripartite schema of economic civilization and Foucault's analytics of power and government. Considering the historical background of modern society, Braudel being an economic historian focuses on the history of capitalism whereas Foucault deals with the history of (neo)liberalism. The paper is finally engaged in integrating their analytics, which also makes the views of Braudel and Foucault on structures and events all the clearer, in order to take further steps towards achieving a comprehensive sociological and historical understanding of capitalism and (neo)liberalism.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{bryanik2025structuralist,
    author = "Bryanik, N. V.",
    title = "Structuralist Conception of the History of Science",
    year = "2025",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Bryanik argues against the common assumption that structuralism is anti-historicist, contending that structuralist methodology inherently combines a synchronic study of a system's internal relations with a diachronic historical analysis characterized by discontinuity and "mutation" rather than linear, sequential change. Drawing on Saussure (linguistics), Propp (folkloristics), and Foucault (life and human sciences), the article likens the historian of science in this tradition to a geologist or, in Foucault's case, an "archaeologist" excavating strata separated by ruptures. RELEVANCE: This paper directly addresses the project's question of whether Foucault should be understood as a structuralist about history, arguing that Foucault's discontinuist archaeology exemplifies rather than contradicts an underlying structuralist historical method shared with other structuralist thinkers. POSITION: Defends the thesis that structuralism has its own coherent, non-linear conception of history organized around synchronic/diachronic complementarity and discontinuity, with Foucault's archaeological method as a paradigm case within the humanities and life sciences.},
    keywords = "structuralism, discontinuity, archaeology, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Since the 60-70s of the twentieth century in the philosophy of science there has been an active search for new approaches to understanding the nature of science, since the claim of logical positivism (as the dominant current in this area of philosophy) to create a real image of science has not justified itself. Appeal to the history of science is seen as the most promising direction in modern science research – the philosophy of science becomes the history of science. The aim of the article is to substantiate the existence in structuralism of an original concept of the history of science, in which the structural method of studying statics/synchrony in the development of science is complemented by the historical analysis of dynamics, including situations of discontinuity/mutations. In order to reveal this concept, it is necessary to overcome the prejudice that even specialists have about anti-historicism/ non-historicism of structuralism. The article substantiates the idea of the organicity of the principle of historicism to structuralism, which is concretised by involving the material on the use and evaluation of the historical method in the studies of F. de Saussure (linguistics), V. Propp (folkloristics), M. Foucault (living and human sciences). Structuralist methodology includes two stages. At the first stage, the object under study is considered in its internal relations and interrelations and its regularities are revealed with the abstraction of the temporal parameter. What is revealed is essentially the internal form of the object, which gave rise to accusations of formalism in structuralism; whereas structuralists present this aspect as statics, synchronicity. The second stage is historical analysis, which takes into account the temporal sequence, dynamics, diachronic aspect. But unlike other historical-scientific concepts, the dynamics of the studied object does not appear as a linear and sequential change of states in time, but as situations of discontinuity, as mutations separating one layer of synchrony from another. Therefore, the historian of science is likened to a geologist (V. Propp) or an archaeologist (M. Foucault) excavating geological or cultural strata. The significance of such a concept of the history of science is that it allows us to address such areas of knowledge, which were clearly underestimated in the positivist tradition of the philosophy of science – the humanities and the sciences of living things, and to raise them to the theoretical level of research. The main methods of the research were: comparative analysis to find out the similarity of structuralist attitudes of domestic and foreign scientists and at different stages of this trend, as well as common features of structuralism in different areas of humanitarian knowledge; on the basis of the logical method the sequence of structural and historical analysis was identified and justified; the critical approach allowed us to overcome the established assessment of non-historicity and formalism of structuralism. The novelty of the findings lies in the identification of the possibilities of structuralist interpretation of the history of science (and not only of the humanities), which is particularly important in the context of the priority focus of modern philosophy of science on the history of science.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{sartori2020genealogycriticaltheory,
    author = "Sartori, Andrew",
    title = "Genealogy, Critical Theory, History",
    year = "2020",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Sartori argues that although Marx was likely not foremost in Foucault's mind when writing "Nietzsche, Genealogy, History" (1971), Foucault's genealogical critique of nineteenth-century historicism is intended to encompass Marx, whom Foucault had already consigned in The Order of Things (1966) to a nineteenth-century epistemic order organized around the convergence of "historicity" with "the human essence." Reading Foucault's critique of historicism against Moishe Postone's postfoundationalist reading of Marx, Sartori argues genealogy's critical purchase does not actually extend to Marx's analysis of social form, which remains available as a resource for radical critique immanent to social form rather than reliant on historicist assumptions. RELEVANCE: This paper directly tests the project's question of Foucault's relation to Marxism by asking whether Foucault's anti-teleological, anti-historicist critique (developed via his reading of Nietzsche) actually succeeds against Marx, concluding that it does not straightforwardly do so -- a finding with direct implications for how "anti-Hegelian"/anti-teleological Foucault's history ultimately is vis-à-vis Marxist materialism. POSITION: Argues that Foucault's Nietzschean genealogical critique of historicism, while intended to include Marx, does not successfully undermine Marx's own (non-historicist) categorial analysis of social form.},
    keywords = "Marxism, Nietzsche, genealogy, historicism, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = {Marx was most likely far from the forefront of Michel Foucault's mind when he wrote "Nietzsche, Genealogy, History" in 1971. Nonetheless, there is little reason to doubt that Foucault considered it as effective a critique of Marx as of any other nineteenth-century historicist. Already in The Order of Things of 1966, Foucault had consigned Marx to a nineteenth-century epistemic order that he characterized in terms of the ultimate convergence of "historicity" with "the human essence." It was in this context that he had made his famous remark that Marxism's debates with bourgeois economics amounted to "no more than storms in a children's paddling pool," insofar as Marx's thought "exists in nineteenth-century thought like a fish in water: that is, it is unable to breathe anywhere else." Given that Foucault would only grow more virulent in his anti-Marxism as the years passed, there can be little doubt that his later genealogical critique was also presumed to encompass Marx in its embrace. This short article represents, as much as anything, the distillate of my experience as someone who came of age at the apex of Foucault's anglophone influence in the late 1980s and 1990s, who taught works by both Foucault and Marx in the Social Sciences Core at the University of Chicago, and who thus had the opportunity to discuss them extensively in weekly instructor meetings under the leadership of Moishe Postone, from 1998 to 2007. What, it asks, does Postone's postfoundationalist and posthistoricist reading of Marx look like when examined through the lens of Foucault's case for Nietzschean genealogy? Foucault's acute critique of historicism makes it possible to read Marx's writings with a sharper eye to their conceptual distance from the variety of nineteenth-century historicism to which Foucault himself consigned them. Insofar as genealogy proves to have no critical purchase on Marx's theoretical approach, however, Marx's analysis of social form remains available to postfoundationalism as a framework that embraces categorial reflexivity as the basis for a radical critique of social domination from a standpoint immanent to social form.},
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{olssen2004foucaultmarxism,
    author = "Olssen, Mark",
    title = "Foucault and Marxism: Rewriting the Theory of Historical Materialism",
    year = "2004",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Olssen argues that despite Foucault's frequent criticism of Marxism, his approach bears striking parallels to it as a method, an account of history, and an analysis of social structure: both treat social practices as transitory and all knowledge and intellectual formations as linked to social relations and power, asserting the historical relativity of all systems, structures, and concepts while not denying a materialism of physical necessities. However, Olssen argues the differences are fundamental: Foucault rejects Hegel's conception of history and society as a unified developing totality, rejects essences and teleology, and rejects any utopian impulse tied to laws of economic development or the historical role of the proletariat, ultimately supporting localized resistance and democratic incrementalism rather than revolutionary or totalizing strategies. RELEVANCE: This paper directly addresses the project's question of Foucault's relation to Marxism and, crucially, ties that relation explicitly to his rejection of Hegelian teleological totality, linking the Marxism and Hegel/dialectic clusters of this domain through a single argument. POSITION: "Rewriting rather than rejection" thesis holding that Foucault's historical method shares Marxism's materialist and anti-essentialist impulses while explicitly repudiating its Hegelian-teleological and utopian-revolutionary commitments.},
    keywords = "Marxism, Hegel, historical-materialism, teleology, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "This article explores the relationship of Foucault to Marxism. Although he was often critical of Marxism, Foucault's own approach bears striking parallels to Marxism, as a form of method, as an account of history, and as an analysis of social structure. Like Marxism, Foucault represents social practices as transitory and all knowledge and intellectual formations as linked to social relations and power. In this he asserts the historical relativity of all systems and structures — of society, of thought, of theory and of concepts, while at the same time not denying a materialism of physical necessities. Yet while Foucault's approach reveals these important similarities to Marxism, the differences, claims the author, are fundamental. These concern his rejection of Hegel's conceptions of history and society as a unified developing totality, his rejection of essences and teleology, and his rejection of any utopian impulse revolving around the laws of economic development or the role of the proletariat in history. Foucault's own conception of change, in fact, is represented in ways that are altogether different to Marx's approach, and ultimately supports localistic forms of resistance and specific forms of democratic incrementalism, rather than revolutionary or totalistic strategies as the basis of transforming society.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{choat2010marxpoststructuralism,
    author = "Choat, Simon",
    title = "Marx Through Post-Structuralism: Lyotard, Derrida, Foucault, Deleuze",
    year = "2010",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Choat examines how four major post-structuralist thinkers -- Lyotard, Derrida, Foucault, and Deleuze -- each read and rework Marx, devoting a dedicated chapter ("The history of the present: Marx through Foucault") to Foucault's specific relation to Marx's thought and historical method, alongside chapters situating all four thinkers within postwar French philosophy's engagement with Marxism. RELEVANCE: As an extended, comparative treatment situating Foucault's post-structuralist historical method specifically against Marx (and against the other major post-structuralists' distinct engagements with Marx), this book supplies the project with a broader comparative framework for assessing how distinctively Foucauldian the "history of the present" is relative to other post-structuralist appropriations of Marxist history. POSITION: Comparative study arguing that post-structuralism, including Foucault, should be understood as offering substantive re-readings of Marx rather than simple rejections, situating Foucault's "history of the present" as one of several post-structuralist re-workings of Marxist historical method.},
    keywords = "Marxism, post-structuralism, history-of-the-present, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "<JATS1:p>Marx Through Post-Structuralism presents a thorough critical examination of the readings of Marx given by four post-structuralist thinkers, all key figures in Continental philosophy: Jean-François Lyotard, Jacques Derrida, Michel Foucault, and Gilles Deleuze. Arguing that both Marx and the post-structuralists seek to produce a genuinely materialist philosophy, the author aims to develop a better understanding of both Marx and post-structuralism and in so doing to reflect on the possibilities and problems for materialist philosophy more broadly.</JATS1:p> <JATS1:p>Against the common assumption that post-structuralism begins with a rejection of Marx, Choat argues that Marx has been a key influence on post-structuralist thought and that each of the four thinkers examined affirms Marx's contemporary significance. By looking at how these thinkers have read Marx - analysing their direct comments, unspoken uses, and implicit criticisms - the book demonstrates that there is a distinct and original post-structuralist approach to Marx that allows us to read him in a new light.</JATS1:p>",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{ryder2013foucaultalthusser,
    author = "Ryder, Andrew",
    title = "Foucault and Althusser: Epistemological Differences with Political Effects",
    journal = "Foucault Studies",
    year = "2013",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Ryder argues that Foucault's relationship to Marxism must be disaggregated: Foucault dismissed a humanist, historicist Marx in The Order of Things but conceded the existence of a more compelling, non-humanist Marxism, for which Louis Althusser's structuralist innovations were crucial. The paper traces how consideration of the Foucault-Althusser relation has varied, examining the specific epistemological differences between the two thinkers and their divergent political effects. RELEVANCE: This paper is a central resource for the project's question of whether Foucault is a "structuralist" about history and knowledge, using the Foucault-Althusser comparison as the test case for how Foucault's archaeology/genealogy relates to Althusserian structural Marxism as opposed to humanist historicism. POSITION: Differentiates Foucault's rejection of humanist/historicist Marxism from his qualified sympathy toward Althusserian structural Marxism, treating epistemological divergences between Foucault and Althusser as generative of distinct political consequences.},
    keywords = "Marxism, Althusser, structuralism, epistemology, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: pages, doi",
    abstract = "Michel Foucault was at times critical of the Marxist tradition, and at other times more sympathetic. After his dismissal of Marx in The Order of Things , he conceded the existence of a more compelling, non-humanist version of this discourse. Louis Althusser’s innovations are crucial for the existence of this second Marxism. While consideration of the relation between Foucault and Althusser varies between those who emphasize relations between State and capital, and conversely those who inscribe Marxist considerations into a micro-political account, the distinction between the two thinkers takes place earlier in the development of their respective outlooks. Foucault initially emphasized Marxism as an anthropological eschatology; he revises this argument, commending the possibility of an epistemological mutation of history inherent in Marx’s thought. I locate crucial distinctions between Foucault and Althusser in the early work of the 1960s as inflecting relations in the seemingly more proximate work of the 1970s. In this approach, we can better examine Foucault’s non-Marxist contentions in order to consider the reciprocal distinctions and contributions between these two forms of anti-humanism, providing the necessary groundwork for debates regarding the nature of subjectivity, the State, and revolution.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@article{riep2025historyoftruth,
    author = "Riep, Leonhard",
    title = {"The History of Truth": Foucault in Buffalo, 1972},
    journal = "Foucault Studies",
    year = "2025",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Based on previously unpublished tape recordings from SUNY Buffalo, Riep reconstructs Foucault's 1972 lecture course "The History of Truth," tracing his archaeological investigation of the discursive practices producing "truth" in Western history through two knowledge-power complexes: the "truth of measure" in ancient Greek law and the "truth of investigation" in medieval law. The article pays particular attention to Foucault's critical remarks on Althusser's variant of "scientific Marxism," situating these remarks within the methodological foundations of his archaeological approach at this transitional moment in his career. RELEVANCE: This paper supplies primary archival evidence of Foucault's direct engagement with (and critical distance from) Althusserian scientific Marxism at a key point of methodological transition (archaeology moving toward genealogy), directly bearing on the project's question of Foucault's relation to Marxism and structuralism. POSITION: Archival reconstruction establishing that Foucault's critique of Althusserian "scientific Marxism" was an explicit, articulated part of his methodological self-positioning in the early 1970s, not merely an implicit or retrospective stance.},
    keywords = "Marxism, Althusser, archaeology, truth, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi",
    abstract = {ABSTRACT: Based on tape recordings preserved in the archives of SUNY Buffalo, this article discusses a hitherto unpublished lecture course entitled "The History of Truth," which Foucault gave in Buffalo in 1972. It traces Foucault's archaeological investigation of the discursive practices that led to the phenomenon known in Western history as "truth." Accordingly, the article reconstructs the two central "knowledge-power" complexes that Foucault analyzes: the "truth of measure" in relation to the question of justice in the ancient Greek legal system and the "truth of investigation" within the discourse on war and the appeasement of society in medieval law. The Buffalo lectures clarify the substantive connection between these topics and also provide deeper insight into the methodological foundations of Foucault's archaeological approach. In this regard, particular attention is paid to his critical remarks on Althusser's variant of scientific Marxism. The article concludes that the audio recordings of the lecture course "The History of Truth" are an important addition to Foucault's first two already published lecture courses at the Collège de France, as they allow listeners to experience Foucault not only as an impressive scholar, but also as a philosophical practitioner.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{megill1990ambiguity,
    author = "Megill, Allan",
    title = "Foucault, Ambiguity, and the Rhetoric of Historiography",
    journal = "History of the Human Sciences",
    year = "1990",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Megill argues that Foucault's histories are constitutively ambiguous between straightforward empirical historiography and a rhetorically driven, philosophically motivated genre, and that this ambiguity is not a defect to be resolved but a structural feature of how Foucault's texts operate on readers - alternately inviting and refusing normal historiographical scrutiny. RELEVANCE: Offers a framework, widely cited in later reception debates, for explaining why historians and philosophers talk past each other when arguing about Foucault's "accuracy": the two camps are implicitly applying different genre expectations to the same texts. Directly addresses this domain's question of whether archaeology/genealogy is usable historical method or imported philosophy. POSITION: Rhetorical/genre-reclassification reading; treats the "was Foucault a historian" dispute as partly a category error.},
    keywords = "historiography, rhetoric, reception, method, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi"
}

@misc{windschuttle1998foucaulthistorian,
    author = "Windschuttle, Keith",
    title = "Foucault as Historian",
    year = "1998",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Windschuttle mounts a sustained empirical prosecution of Foucault's historical practice, arguing that across Madness and Civilization, Discipline and Punish, and The History of Sexuality Foucault repeatedly misrepresented, selectively quoted, or simply invented evidence to fit a predetermined thesis about power and exclusion, and that professional historians who have adopted Foucauldian method have imported these errors uncritically. RELEVANCE: The most uncompromising empirical-accuracy critique found in this domain; provides the concrete, itemized objections (chronology of confinement, treatment of sources, selective citation) that the research idea's "were his histories accurate?" question specifically asks for. Later expanded into Windschuttle's broader polemic against postmodern historiography in The Killing of History. POSITION: Empirical/archival rejection; represents the harshest pole of historian reception, explicitly denying Foucault standing as a historian.},
    keywords = "historiography, empirical-accuracy, madness, discipline-and-punish, critique, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@article{scull1990foucaultmadness,
    author = "Scull, Andrew",
    title = "Michel Foucault's History of Madness",
    journal = "History of the Human Sciences",
    year = "1990",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Scull, a historian of psychiatry, revisits Histoire de la folie on the occasion of renewed English-language debate and argues that Foucault's account of a unitary "Great Confinement" and his romanticized opposition of madness to reason do not survive confrontation with the archival and comparative evidence historians of the asylum have since assembled, even as the book's rhetorical and conceptual force helped create the field of the history of psychiatry as it now exists. RELEVANCE: A leading historian of madness/psychiatry assessing Foucault's founding text from inside the discipline it helped create; central to the "empirical accuracy" controversy and to the appropriation/rejection tension this domain tracks. POSITION: Empirical/archival rejection combined with acknowledgment of field-founding influence; a historian's mixed verdict rather than wholesale dismissal.},
    keywords = "madness, history-of-psychiatry, empirical-accuracy, reception, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi"
}

@article{lacapra1990foucaulthistorymadness,
    author = "LaCapra, Dominick",
    title = "Foucault, History, and Madness",
    journal = "History of the Human Sciences",
    year = "1990",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: LaCapra, an intellectual historian, defends a more sympathetic reading of Foucault's history of madness against purely empiricist objections, arguing that Foucault's text operates simultaneously as documentary history and as a self-consciously "excessive" or transgressive form of writing that deliberately unsettles standard historiographical protocols, and that judging it solely by archival-accuracy criteria misses this dual character. RELEVANCE: Represents the intellectual-historian counterweight to Windschuttle- and Scull-style empirical prosecution within the same 1990 special issue, illustrating the split among professional historians themselves over whether archaeology/genealogy should be judged as history or as a hybrid historical-philosophical genre. POSITION: Selective appropriation / genre-reclassification; a historian of ideas defending Foucault's method against strict empiricist standards. METADATA NOTE: The author name is truncated in both CrossRef and Semantic Scholar source metadata to "Dominick L."; a corroborating OpenAlex author record for this article lists an institutional affiliation of Cornell University, consistent with the intellectual historian Dominick LaCapra, but this attribution is flagged here for transparency rather than silently corrected.},
    keywords = "madness, intellectual-history, reception, method, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi"
}

